ent":"general-purpose","attributionMcpServer":"plugin:context-mode:context-mode","attributionMcpTool":"ctx_execute","type":"assistant","uuid":"c9728938-bc38-4f68-9344-8dac91b0f620","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:22:09.734Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"c9728938-bc38-4f68-9344-8dac91b0f620","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FfGgMgDNWFAqFuKrPMfHz3","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```python\nimport json,re,os\nd=json.load(open(os.path.expanduser('~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.json')))\npat=re.compile(r'Tech_Inventory|SoftwareTech|Curricul|RTP|Return to|HPI|Manual|NCAA|Toby|Education',re.I)\nfor sec in ['projects','ideas','inbox']:\n  for e in d.get(sec,[]):\n    t=json.dumps(e)\n    if pat.search(t):\n      e2=dict(e)\n      ups=e2.pop('updates',None)\n      print('\\n---',sec, json.dumps(e2)[:1500])\n      if ups:\n        for u in ups[:4]: print('   UPD',u.get('date'),str(u.get('note'))[:700])\n\n```\n\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"Tech_Inventory_Analysis\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 16, \"status\": \"active\", \"stage\": \"building\", \"purpose\": \"Clean and analyze the Baylor Athletics Technology Inventory survey (Microsoft Forms Quick Inventory, 5 questions). Turns free-text departmental answers into a tidy one-row-per-technology inventory, a coverage map against the 26 sport/unit list, and a screening list of athlete-data systems that feeds the Detailed 17-question second pass. Data collection companion to SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework, which holds the framework, form specs and form links.\", \"next_action\": \"Follow up with the five outstanding units before the 29 September department soft deadline - Golf (M), Tennis (M), Tennis (W), Volleyball, Business Office. Then re-export, re-run the pipeline, and every document rebuilds with final numbers. Still unidentified: SCRL app and JABA.\", \"dev_folder\": \"Tech_Inventory_Analysis\", \"links\": [\"https://claude.ai/code/artifact/42a7b360-2add-41f3-9d5f-2cc5f17141c8\"], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-30T11:38:35\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-30 Added a Word version (scripts/build_docx.py -> Tech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.docx, 8 data tables built from the CSVs so it cannot drift, Baylor-green headings, branding in the header and document status on every page per Kenny's review history). Reviewed the meeting transcripts for what deliverable Kenny actually wants and found two things. First, a correction: 29 August was Applied Performance's own deadline, but the 2026-08-24 all-staff meeting set a department-wide SOFT DEADLINE of 29 SEPTEMBER with unit leaders assigning staff - so collection is still open and the five non-responding units are outstanding, not delinquent. The report, PDF, HTML and markdown were all reframed as an\n   UPD 2026-08-29 Delivered to OneDrive alongside the framework documents at 00_Primary_Storage_Drive/05_SYSTEMS/SoftwareTechDocs/TechInventory_2026-08/ - a 10-page print-ready PDF (Tech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.pdf, Letter portrait, background graphics preserved, no browser header or file path stamped in the margins), a standalone HTML copy, the markdown source, and the six clean CSVs under data/. Filenames follow the existing SoftwareTechDocs _ER_<date> convention. Copies, not moves: originals stay in ~/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis and checksums were verified to match. Raw exports were deliberately not copied - they carry respondent names and email addresses, and the report itself was scanned to conf\n   UPD 2026-08-29 Published the findings as a shareable report page (deliverables/findings_report.html, artifact 42a7b360) - Baylor green palette, coverage grid keyed to response count, category and shared-product bar charts, light and dark themes. Eric answered the three open questions: 'Marketing and Fan Experience' is the Marketing department's current name, so the two merge and Marketing is no longer a coverage gap (33 units, not 34); Red Card is the student-athlete meal-money stipend redeemable at selected local vendors, so it now has its own athlete-stipend category and counts as athlete data (60 candidates, not 59); SCRL app and JABA, both from Brand Engagement, remain unidentified and are the last two\n   UPD 2026-08-29 Re-ran against the post-deadline export (37 responses, up from 15). Two Microsoft Forms traps found and handled in the pipeline: the export declares a bogus A1:A1 sheet dimension so openpyxl's read_only reader returns an empty sheet, and the new export inserted a 'Last modified time' column that shifted every question one place right - columns are now located by header text, never by position. Also fixed a real parser bug the new data exposed: comma lists containing parentheses were never split, which had collapsed the entire Broadcast and Big Screens response (13 vendors: Ross Video, Evertz, Grass Valley, Calrec, Adder, EditShare, Riedel, Daktronics, ANC, Haivision, Vyvx, LTN, Switch) into \n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 12, \"status\": \"active\", \"stage\": \"building\", \"purpose\": \"Software and Technology Management Framework for Baylor Athletics: a 3-phase request/approval/review process plus athlete data categorization, ownership and protection. From the 2026-08-03 Kenny Boyd whiteboard session; drafted for the 2026-08-10 stakeholder meeting with John Allen and Micah.\", \"next_action\": \"Send the draft package to Kenny Boyd by Fri 2026-08-07, ahead of the Mon 2026-08-10 presentation with John Allen and Micah\", \"dev_folder\": \"SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-15T17:06:07\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-15 Curation 2026-08-15: A real project — a task I was asked to develop.\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Built the full Monday package from the Kenny Boyd whiteboard, the 54-min Fireflies transcript, and the Sport_Tech_Vetting reference library. Five deliverables in Word/Excel: framework doc (8pp, 3 phases + data FAQ appendix + open-decisions appendix), workflow diagram, Technology Idea Form, internal ATH Form, tiered evaluation scorecard, and the ITS standing-meeting request tracker. Key move: mapped Kenny's 3 phases onto the NCAA Sports Science Institute guidance on Responsible Use of Performance Technologies (surfaced via the Eversheds Sutherland one-pager Kenny asked to test against), reframing the framework from internal preference to NCAA-aligned implementation. Scorecard extends the exis\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Added an editable Excalidraw version of the workflow diagram (Workflow_Diagram.excalidraw) with bound labels and bound arrows, plus a 1026x1700 PNG export. Verified by rendering in excalidraw.com: JSON valid, all containerId/boundElements/arrow bindings resolve, full coverage, no shape overlaps; fixed a band-label/arrow collision and low-contrast note text found in the render. Word doc keeps the matplotlib diagram to match its proportional typography; the Excalidraw file is the working copy for whiteboarding with Kenny's group.\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Draft package reviewed by Eric; no changes requested at review. Framework doc, workflow diagram (matplotlib in-doc + editable Excalidraw working copy), both intake forms, tiered scorecard and ITS request tracker are all final for the Friday send.\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"NCAA_BestPractices_Doc\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 2, \"status\": \"active\", \"purpose\": \"NCAA best practices document (Chris Ruf meeting deliverable)\", \"stage\": \"idea\", \"next_action\": \"Collect source material and set the doc skeleton\", \"dev_folder\": \"NCAA_BestPractices_Doc\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-04T14:14:41\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Confirmed as a separate project from SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework — related scope, but not merged. That framework already covers the NCAA SSI recommendations on implementing new technologies (its Phase 1) and continual improvement (its Phase 3), and makes a first pass at data rights and student-athlete education, so this doc can reference it for the request/approval process instead of restating it.\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"Sport_Tech_Vetting\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 13, \"status\": \"paused\", \"stage\": \"shipped\", \"purpose\": \"Tech vetting process and framework discussed with Chris Ruf\", \"next_action\": \"Extract the vendor-collateral pattern from the Shiftwave build into the one-page vetting framework draft\", \"dev_folder\": \"Sport_Tech_Vetting\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-15T17:06:07\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-15 Curation 2026-08-15: Has kind of morphed into the SoftwareTech management framework. There are still items in there that we'll reference, but it's not currently active — it's more from that other project.\n   UPD 2026-08-03 Shiftwave ran as the framework's first live test case (unsolicited vendor pitch needing a yes/no). Shipped the athlete-facing collateral: 3 design directions built, duration-first matrix chosen, laminated protocol card with press-ready bleed PDF and print/lamination spec, plus a 5-slide staff deck. Produced a reusable vendor-collateral pattern: reproduce vendor content verbatim, label your own additions as yours, and write down the selection rule so it stays correctable. Also built a render-audit harness (contrast, print point size, hierarchy ratio, overflow). Harvested to SecondBrain as 3 Knowledge/frameworks articles.\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"SportScience_Curriculum\", \"lane\": \"side\", \"rank\": 2, \"status\": \"active\", \"purpose\": \"15-16 week sport science curriculum for students, built from existing resource docs and study materials\", \"stage\": \"idea\", \"next_action\": \"Sketch the week-by-week topic map\", \"dev_folder\": \"CPSS_BaylorSportScience_Curriculum\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-07-31T12:00:00\"}\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"CFB_Analytics_Lab\", \"lane\": \"side\", \"rank\": 6, \"status\": \"active\", \"stage\": \"building\", \"purpose\": \"A learning workspace for fluency in applying data-science techniques to football data \\u2014 the methods in Eager & Erickson's Football Analytics with Python and R, transposed from the book's NFL/nflfastR data onto college football data from the CollegeFootballData API. Fluency means choosing a method for a football question, fitting it, and explaining both what it says and where it breaks; each lesson ends in a drill completed unaided, and the drill is the evidence. Curriculum: over-expected metrics (CPOE/RYOE), GLMs, stability analysis, clustering + PCA, tree ensembles, and Bayesian hierarchical models (lesson 0007, a deliberate extension past the book). Explicitly out of scope: beating the betting market, Baylor coaching deliverables, and any change to CFB_Victory_Model.\", \"next_action\": \"Review and merge PR #1, open since 2026-08-13 \\u2014 the `foundation` branch, 21 commits ahead of `main`, with the three fix-before-merge rulings already applied in 02a190b \\u2014 then complete Drill 0001 and report the result; lesson 0002 is gated on that drill\", \"dev_folder\": \"CFB_Analytics_Lab\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-15T17:06:07\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-15 Curation 2026-08-15: A professional development project on the side to enhance my data science and analytical skills.\n   UPD 2026-08-13 Foundation built end-to-end on branch `foundation` (20 commits, unmerged): 1,495,005 plays pulled across 2019-2025 in 129 CFBD API calls, 49 tests passing offline, FBS reconciliation against CFBD's own game list matching to 99.98% with zero join inflation. Nine-task plan executed under the superpowers SDD flow with a spec, a plan, and a durable execution record capturing all 20 rulings made without a human in the loop — written so any wrong call can be found and undone. Three fix-before-merge findings were caught and repaired in review: (17) `/plays` ships FCS games mixed with FBS and CFBD's coverage changes shape at the 2022 boundary, so every cross-2022 analysis in the curriculum was quiet\n\n--- ideas {\"captured_at\": \"2026-08-18T19:27:06\", \"pitch\": \"Intern curriculum development agent: build the intern curriculum from CPSS content, organized on the structural principles in the Clemson sample work\", \"source\": \"chat capturing three agent-execution project ideas\", \"status\": \"inbox\"}\n\n--- ideas {\"captured_at\": \"2026-08-18T19:27:10\", \"pitch\": \"Course-builder agent on Matt Pocock teach skills: courses on ecological dynamics, constraints-based learning, and a constraints-led approach to return to performance, to walk AT Joe Smith through\", \"source\": \"chat capturing three agent-execution project ideas\", \"status\": \"inbox\"}\n\n--- inbox {\"name\": \"RTP_DataCollection_Talk\", \"dev_folder\": \"RTP_DataCollection_Talk\", \"source\": \"curation-merge-2026-08-15\", \"status\": \"paused\"}\n\n--- inbox {\"name\": \"TechEd_Prop\", \"dev_folder\": \"TechEd_Prop\", \"source\": \"curation-merge-2026-08-15\", \"status\": \"active\", \"purpose\": \"Our education initiative \\u2014 building out one-on-one pagers or short slide decks.\"}\n"}]}]},"uuid":"c8c1c605-9dc1-4b86-a150-9c6a276bdd2a","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:22:11.229Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"```python\nimport json,re,os\nd=json.load(open(os.path.expanduser('~/DEV/project-triage/portfolio.json')))\npat=re.compile(r'Tech_Inventory|SoftwareTech|Curricul|RTP|Return to|HPI|Manual|NCAA|Toby|Education',re.I)\nfor sec in ['projects','ideas','inbox']:\n  for e in d.get(sec,[]):\n    t=json.dumps(e)\n    if pat.search(t):\n      e2=dict(e)\n      ups=e2.pop('updates',None)\n      print('\\n---',sec, json.dumps(e2)[:1500])\n      if ups:\n        for u in ups[:4]: print('   UPD',u.get('date'),str(u.get('note'))[:700])\n\n```\n\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"Tech_Inventory_Analysis\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 16, \"status\": \"active\", \"stage\": \"building\", \"purpose\": \"Clean and analyze the Baylor Athletics Technology Inventory survey (Microsoft Forms Quick Inventory, 5 questions). Turns free-text departmental answers into a tidy one-row-per-technology inventory, a coverage map against the 26 sport/unit list, and a screening list of athlete-data systems that feeds the Detailed 17-question second pass. Data collection companion to SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework, which holds the framework, form specs and form links.\", \"next_action\": \"Follow up with the five outstanding units before the 29 September department soft deadline - Golf (M), Tennis (M), Tennis (W), Volleyball, Business Office. Then re-export, re-run the pipeline, and every document rebuilds with final numbers. Still unidentified: SCRL app and JABA.\", \"dev_folder\": \"Tech_Inventory_Analysis\", \"links\": [\"https://claude.ai/code/artifact/42a7b360-2add-41f3-9d5f-2cc5f17141c8\"], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-30T11:38:35\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-30 Added a Word version (scripts/build_docx.py -> Tech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.docx, 8 data tables built from the CSVs so it cannot drift, Baylor-green headings, branding in the header and document status on every page per Kenny's review history). Reviewed the meeting transcripts for what deliverable Kenny actually wants and found two things. First, a correction: 29 August was Applied Performance's own deadline, but the 2026-08-24 all-staff meeting set a department-wide SOFT DEADLINE of 29 SEPTEMBER with unit leaders assigning staff - so collection is still open and the five non-responding units are outstanding, not delinquent. The report, PDF, HTML and markdown were all reframed as an\n   UPD 2026-08-29 Delivered to OneDrive alongside the framework documents at 00_Primary_Storage_Drive/05_SYSTEMS/SoftwareTechDocs/TechInventory_2026-08/ - a 10-page print-ready PDF (Tech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.pdf, Letter portrait, background graphics preserved, no browser header or file path stamped in the margins), a standalone HTML copy, the markdown source, and the six clean CSVs under data/. Filenames follow the existing SoftwareTechDocs _ER_<date> convention. Copies, not moves: originals stay in ~/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis and checksums were verified to match. Raw exports were deliberately not copied - they carry respondent names and email addresses, and the report itself was scanned to conf\n   UPD 2026-08-29 Published the findings as a shareable report page (deliverables/findings_report.html, artifact 42a7b360) - Baylor green palette, coverage grid keyed to response count, category and shared-product bar charts, light and dark themes. Eric answered the three open questions: 'Marketing and Fan Experience' is the Marketing department's current name, so the two merge and Marketing is no longer a coverage gap (33 units, not 34); Red Card is the student-athlete meal-money stipend redeemable at selected local vendors, so it now has its own athlete-stipend category and counts as athlete data (60 candidates, not 59); SCRL app and JABA, both from Brand Engagement, remain unidentified and are the last two\n   UPD 2026-08-29 Re-ran against the post-deadline export (37 responses, up from 15). Two Microsoft Forms traps found and handled in the pipeline: the export declares a bogus A1:A1 sheet dimension so openpyxl's read_only reader returns an empty sheet, and the new export inserted a 'Last modified time' column that shifted every question one place right - columns are now located by header text, never by position. Also fixed a real parser bug the new data exposed: comma lists containing parentheses were never split, which had collapsed the entire Broadcast and Big Screens response (13 vendors: Ross Video, Evertz, Grass Valley, Calrec, Adder, EditShare, Riedel, Daktronics, ANC, Haivision, Vyvx, LTN, Switch) into \n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 12, \"status\": \"active\", \"stage\": \"building\", \"purpose\": \"Software and Technology Management Framework for Baylor Athletics: a 3-phase request/approval/review process plus athlete data categorization, ownership and protection. From the 2026-08-03 Kenny Boyd whiteboard session; drafted for the 2026-08-10 stakeholder meeting with John Allen and Micah.\", \"next_action\": \"Send the draft package to Kenny Boyd by Fri 2026-08-07, ahead of the Mon 2026-08-10 presentation with John Allen and Micah\", \"dev_folder\": \"SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-15T17:06:07\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-15 Curation 2026-08-15: A real project — a task I was asked to develop.\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Built the full Monday package from the Kenny Boyd whiteboard, the 54-min Fireflies transcript, and the Sport_Tech_Vetting reference library. Five deliverables in Word/Excel: framework doc (8pp, 3 phases + data FAQ appendix + open-decisions appendix), workflow diagram, Technology Idea Form, internal ATH Form, tiered evaluation scorecard, and the ITS standing-meeting request tracker. Key move: mapped Kenny's 3 phases onto the NCAA Sports Science Institute guidance on Responsible Use of Performance Technologies (surfaced via the Eversheds Sutherland one-pager Kenny asked to test against), reframing the framework from internal preference to NCAA-aligned implementation. Scorecard extends the exis\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Added an editable Excalidraw version of the workflow diagram (Workflow_Diagram.excalidraw) with bound labels and bound arrows, plus a 1026x1700 PNG export. Verified by rendering in excalidraw.com: JSON valid, all containerId/boundElements/arrow bindings resolve, full coverage, no shape overlaps; fixed a band-label/arrow collision and low-contrast note text found in the render. Word doc keeps the matplotlib diagram to match its proportional typography; the Excalidraw file is the working copy for whiteboarding with Kenny's group.\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Draft package reviewed by Eric; no changes requested at review. Framework doc, workflow diagram (matplotlib in-doc + editable Excalidraw working copy), both intake forms, tiered scorecard and ITS request tracker are all final for the Friday send.\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"NCAA_BestPractices_Doc\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 2, \"status\": \"active\", \"purpose\": \"NCAA best practices document (Chris Ruf meeting deliverable)\", \"stage\": \"idea\", \"next_action\": \"Collect source material and set the doc skeleton\", \"dev_folder\": \"NCAA_BestPractices_Doc\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-04T14:14:41\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-04 Confirmed as a separate project from SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework — related scope, but not merged. That framework already covers the NCAA SSI recommendations on implementing new technologies (its Phase 1) and continual improvement (its Phase 3), and makes a first pass at data rights and student-athlete education, so this doc can reference it for the request/approval process instead of restating it.\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"Sport_Tech_Vetting\", \"lane\": \"job\", \"rank\": 13, \"status\": \"paused\", \"stage\": \"shipped\", \"purpose\": \"Tech vetting process and framework discussed with Chris Ruf\", \"next_action\": \"Extract the vendor-collateral pattern from the Shiftwave build into the one-page vetting framework draft\", \"dev_folder\": \"Sport_Tech_Vetting\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-15T17:06:07\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-15 Curation 2026-08-15: Has kind of morphed into the SoftwareTech management framework. There are still items in there that we'll reference, but it's not currently active — it's more from that other project.\n   UPD 2026-08-03 Shiftwave ran as the framework's first live test case (unsolicited vendor pitch needing a yes/no). Shipped the athlete-facing collateral: 3 design directions built, duration-first matrix chosen, laminated protocol card with press-ready bleed PDF and print/lamination spec, plus a 5-slide staff deck. Produced a reusable vendor-collateral pattern: reproduce vendor content verbatim, label your own additions as yours, and write down the selection rule so it stays correctable. Also built a render-audit harness (contrast, print point size, hierarchy ratio, overflow). Harvested to SecondBrain as 3 Knowledge/frameworks articles.\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"SportScience_Curriculum\", \"lane\": \"side\", \"rank\": 2, \"status\": \"active\", \"purpose\": \"15-16 week sport science curriculum for students, built from existing resource docs and study materials\", \"stage\": \"idea\", \"next_action\": \"Sketch the week-by-week topic map\", \"dev_folder\": \"CPSS_BaylorSportScience_Curriculum\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-07-31T12:00:00\"}\n\n--- projects {\"name\": \"CFB_Analytics_Lab\", \"lane\": \"side\", \"rank\": 6, \"status\": \"active\", \"stage\": \"building\", \"purpose\": \"A learning workspace for fluency in applying data-science techniques to football data \\u2014 the methods in Eager & Erickson's Football Analytics with Python and R, transposed from the book's NFL/nflfastR data onto college football data from the CollegeFootballData API. Fluency means choosing a method for a football question, fitting it, and explaining both what it says and where it breaks; each lesson ends in a drill completed unaided, and the drill is the evidence. Curriculum: over-expected metrics (CPOE/RYOE), GLMs, stability analysis, clustering + PCA, tree ensembles, and Bayesian hierarchical models (lesson 0007, a deliberate extension past the book). Explicitly out of scope: beating the betting market, Baylor coaching deliverables, and any change to CFB_Victory_Model.\", \"next_action\": \"Review and merge PR #1, open since 2026-08-13 \\u2014 the `foundation` branch, 21 commits ahead of `main`, with the three fix-before-merge rulings already applied in 02a190b \\u2014 then complete Drill 0001 and report the result; lesson 0002 is gated on that drill\", \"dev_folder\": \"CFB_Analytics_Lab\", \"links\": [], \"curated_at\": \"2026-08-15T17:06:07\"}\n   UPD 2026-08-15 Curation 2026-08-15: A professional development project on the side to enhance my data science and analytical skills.\n   UPD 2026-08-13 Foundation built end-to-end on branch `foundation` (20 commits, unmerged): 1,495,005 plays pulled across 2019-2025 in 129 CFBD API calls, 49 tests passing offline, FBS reconciliation against CFBD's own game list matching to 99.98% with zero join inflation. Nine-task plan executed under the superpowers SDD flow with a spec, a plan, and a durable execution record capturing all 20 rulings made without a human in the loop — written so any wrong call can be found and undone. Three fix-before-merge findings were caught and repaired in review: (17) `/plays` ships FCS games mixed with FBS and CFBD's coverage changes shape at the 2022 boundary, so every cross-2022 analysis in the curriculum was quiet\n\n--- ideas {\"captured_at\": \"2026-08-18T19:27:06\", \"pitch\": \"Intern curriculum development agent: build the intern curriculum from CPSS content, organized on the structural principles in the Clemson sample work\", \"source\": \"chat capturing three agent-execution project ideas\", \"status\": \"inbox\"}\n\n--- ideas {\"captured_at\": \"2026-08-18T19:27:10\", \"pitch\": \"Course-builder agent on Matt Pocock teach skills: courses on ecological dynamics, constraints-based learning, and a constraints-led approach to return to performance, to walk AT Joe Smith through\", \"source\": \"chat capturing three agent-execution project ideas\", \"status\": \"inbox\"}\n\n--- inbox {\"name\": \"RTP_DataCollection_Talk\", \"dev_folder\": \"RTP_DataCollection_Talk\", \"source\": \"curation-merge-2026-08-15\", \"status\": \"paused\"}\n\n--- inbox {\"name\": \"TechEd_Prop\", \"dev_folder\": \"TechEd_Prop\", \"source\": \"curation-merge-2026-08-15\", \"status\": \"active\", \"purpose\": \"Our education initiative \\u2014 building out one-on-one pagers or short slide decks.\"}\n"}],"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c9728938-bc38-4f68-9344-8dac91b0f620","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
