{"parentUuid":"be1df91c-e850-4ee7-b509-27f649f622ed","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DRQae6Nhe9YPHA7zt9fpAh","type":"tool_result","content":"######## meetings/2026-09-09_NCAA_SSI_Webinar__Performance_Tech__Data_Governance.md\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n\n# NCAA SSI Webinar — Performance Tech & Data Governance\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-09 11:00\n**Duration:** 45m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Responsible Technology Use:** Balance innovation with athlete health; set policies, educate, secure data, and regularly review technology impact.\n\n- **Technology as a Tool:** Use wearables and sensors to guide decisions, not replace context-aware judgment; avoid data overload and misuse.\n\n- **Data Privacy and Security:** Consult legal teams on data laws; ensure secure storage and clear athlete consent; watch for vendor data selling.\n\n- **Stakeholder Education:** Train staff deeply on data meaning; keep coaches informed without overwhelming; fully educate athletes and obtain consent.\n\n- **Technology Limitations:** No single metric reliably predicts performance; be wary of opaque algorithms; consider mental health effects of data use.\n\n- **Strategic Integration:** Link medical and performance data for injury prevention; continuously evaluate tech value and foster multidisciplinary collaboration.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Tim Coffey**\nEngage with student athletes to gather feedback on their experiences and perceptions regarding performance technologies and data use (38:18)\nEncourage continuous education of performance and sports medicine staff on validity, reliability, and interpretation of performance technology data (21:46)\n\n**Mark Koberly**\nDevelop and maintain a written plan/policy framework for performance technology adoption including justification, validity checks, data privacy, and access protocols (13:37)\nCoordinate with institutional general counsel, IT, and risk management to ascertain legal and security compliance in data collection, storage, and use (31:06)\nImplement routine reevaluations of performance technology effectiveness based on actionable outcomes and stakeholder feedback (17:22)\n\n**Tim Coffey and Mark Koberly**\nPromote multidisciplinary stakeholder education including coaches and student athletes before deploying new technology (21:46)\nAdvocate for transparency and consent regarding data collection and use with student athletes to mitigate anxiety and privacy concerns (20:16)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nperformance technology, student athlete data, data privacy, educational programs, injury prevention, policy development\n\n######## meetings/2026-09-10_Sep_10_0937_AM.md\n## Project tags\n> Auto-tagged from `data/meeting-topics.json`. Edit freely - add what was missed, delete what is wrong. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n# Sep 10, 09:37 AM\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-10 09:40\n**Duration:** 51m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Athlete Health Priority:** Immediate medical care given during athlete distress; heat acclimation stressed for matches with temps near 97°F.\n\n- **Training Adjustments:** New schedules with lifting days, rest days, and veteran leadership to integrate transfers and manage larger rosters.\n\n- **Equipment Upgrades:** Approved purchase of Kaiser leg press A400 model for advanced metrics; garage door repaired after equipment storage damage.\n\n- **Data Use Caution:** Emphasis on balanced tech use, athlete consent, and education to prevent data overload and mental stress.\n\n- **Staff Changes:** Kevin Gall left, duties shifted; new consultant Joe seeks medical record access with privacy concerns noted.\n\n- **Competition Updates:** Cross country finished 3rd–4th; equestrian meet and track championships upcoming; volleyball and softball easing into season.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Unassigned**\nCommunicate and coordinate athlete seating and lifting logistics with coach for seasonal starts and Sunday match (00:00)\nMonitor athlete with prosthetic leg carefully and avoid unnecessary training modifications unless medically indicated (02:16)\nPrepare cross-country athletes for next meet at Texas A&M and continue with regular/modified training cards (03:43)\nImplement impact tracking pilot with five tumblers and collect data for volume insights (07:19)\nContinue managing increased equestrian roster and prepare for first meet against South Dakota at 11am (08:48)\nManage sprinters and hurdlers practicing independently during coaching absence, focusing on heat safety (13:10)\nManage softball and volleyball post-county fair training and schedule; address health concerns and equipment issues (17:31)\nReinforce equipment storage protocols to avoid damage and secure cords and dumbbells against theft (22:27)\nMaintain awareness of mental health resources and schedule breaks; consider participating in 911 stair climb fundraiser (24:14)\nIntegrate learnings from NCAA webinar on technology use and educate athletes about data privacy, accuracy, and mental health impact (26:06)\nEducate athletes using wearable tech on limitations, especially regarding sleep and heart rate variability data to reduce negative psychological effects (31:47)\nPrepare for installation of new Kaiser leg press, considering location logistics and evaluating cost benefit of advanced model (36:05)\nRSVP to upcoming Chipotle tailgate event as needed (41:42)\nRedistribute responsibilities for staff roles formerly managed by Kevin Gall, mainly among Cali and Kendall (43:24)\nCoordinate with external rehab provider Joe regarding data sharing and medical record integration to avoid duplicate documentation (49:19)\nFinalize soccer team travel and match attendance schedule, managing upcoming games and trips (50:47)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nathlete management, training adaptations, wearable technology, injury prevention, team health incidents, equipment maintenance\n\n\n######## meetings/2026-09-14_Team_Sync__Hiring_Process_ITS__TB_Updates.md\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n# Team Sync — Hiring Process, ITS & TB Updates\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-14 11:30\n**Duration:** 42m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Hiring Process:** Structured checkpoints enhance quality hires; leadership roles need strong mission alignment; student employee shifts require formal approvals.  \n- **Event Experience:** New lighting, fireworks improve fan atmosphere; faith-based elements well received but need better player prep; staff support boosts team culture.  \n- **Student Survey Engagement:** Only 7 of 500 athletes responded; anonymity assured; next surveys target gratitude and services; Teamworks cleanup underway with 4,000+ profiles.  \n- **IT Support Issues:** Slow IT response frustrates staff; admin password removal hampers software installs; single contact and escalation path set to improve outcomes.  \n- **Health & Safety:** Two athletes tested as TB close contacts but symptom-free; staff member’s child tested positive, all cleared; monitoring and routine screening active.  \n- **Communication Tools:** Microsoft Teams promoted for updates; website improvements planned with multimedia content; campus partnerships tracked for reports and visibility.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Unassigned**\nHolly to return to office later this week to assist with operations (00:25)\nHiring committee to continue interviewing and assessing candidates for associate director of NIL education (00:25)\nStaff to provide feedback on bank service complaints, especially regarding football players’ use of to-go boxes (04:10)\nCoaches and liaisons to monitor and elevate academic concerns from initial grade reports (05:15)\nContinue promoting mental health check-ins with student athletes to improve completion rates (06:00)\nSupervisors to conduct one-on-one performance reviews with direct reports before December scheduling conflicts arise, with proper documentation (21:45)\nAll staff encouraged to actively use Microsoft Teams channel for internal communications about events, deadlines, and student athlete engagement (22:24)\nCoordinate with Cody Soto to update multimedia content for the Baylor Athletics website (23:45)\nResolve administrative password access issues to enable efficient software use on staff laptops (33:45)\nFinalize and circulate NCAA attestation report draft for review by end of September; ensure timely submission by mid-October (36:40)\nDocument and track campus collaboration efforts for reporting purposes (37:50)\nInform and provide support for athletes concerning TB testing protocols; disseminate symptom information to appropriate staff (39:10)\nDistribute Parents Weekend schedule and details to relevant personnel (41:50)\n\n**Josh Barnhill**\nStaff to meet with new baseball trainer and athletic case manager as part of orientation (06:00)\n\n**Hiring Managers, Direct Reports**\nHiring managers to adhere to outlined personnel hiring process: initial planning, mid-cycle check-ins, finalist plan, and pre-offer communication with leadership (14:16)\n\n**Malcolm and others**\nTeam leaders to promote Real Response survey participation using QR codes and in meetings (25:45)\n\n**Eric Rash**\nEric Rash to continue managing communications with ITS and escalate unresolved issues as needed; Jovan to copy leadership on ongoing ITS issues (30:40)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nHiring process, Mental health check-ins, NCAA attestation, TB testing, Student athlete engagement, ITS communication\n\n######## status-log/2026-09-14.md\n---\ndate: 2026-09-14\nsports: [Volleyball, Men's Tennis, Football]\n---\n\n## Equipment & installs\n- Nothing changed hands or was installed.\n\n## People & conversations\n- Joe Reed (consultant, Men's Tennis) — met with him last week.\n- Football — helped collect jumps today.\n- Athletic training staff — made the rounds.\n- Student services staff meeting — attended.\n- Micah Lamb — sent a follow-up email about the tech review process.\n- Tech inventory — got the laggard departments to fill it out. Volleyball is the only one outstanding.\n\n## Waiting on / risks\n- Micah Lamb — no response on the tech review process, about 4 weeks now.\n- Waiting on ITS to reply about our tech approval framework.\n- Going to meet with Toby Brooks to help build out the education curriculum for Applied Performance.\n######## status-log/2026-09-09.md\n---\ndate: 2026-09-09\nsports: [Women's Basketball, Acrobatics & Tumbling]\n---\n\n## Equipment & installs\n- Nothing new arrived or got installed. Follow-up work only:\n  - Joe Reed consultant — chasing that down.\n  - Kenny figuring out what's going on with the Polar heart rate monitors (Women's Basketball).\n  - FirstBeat review.\n\n## People & conversations\n- Sat in on the NCAA Sport Science Initiative (SSI) webinar about data and technology in collegiate sport.\n- Landon — brainstormed while organizing my AI agentic setup.\n- Acro & Tumbling staff (meeting was 09-08, captured here on 09-09 from the transcript) — planning an impact-counting system for tumbling load. Five-athlete pilot, manual tracking to start, wearables later once budget allows. **I own the build:** an Excel \"pass builder\" that maps tumbling routines to cumulative impact load. Waiting on them for the skill inventory with per-skill hand/foot impact counts, the daily practice assignments, and a consistent skill naming convention.\n  Source: `Inbox/meetings/2026-09-08_Acro__Tumbling__Impact_Load_Tracking_Pilot.md`\n\n## Waiting on / risks\n- IT things to chase down.\n- Tech inventory — waiting on the people who need to get back to me.\n- Education initiative needs real progress.\n######## 2026-09-14-interview.md\n---\ntype: interview\ndate: 2026-09-14\nduration_min: 15\nthemes: [prioritization, finishing-work, OKRs, three-pillars, productivity-system]\nnotes_produced: [\"[[finish-one-project-before-you-start-the-next]]\"]\npulled_sources: []\n---\n\n# Interview — 2026-09-14\n\nSupervisor log for today: `Inbox/status-log/2026-09-14.md`\n\n## 1. Orientation — What did you spend time on today?\n\nHelped football collect jumps, then made the rounds with the athletic training staff. Went to a student services staff meeting. Then tried to map out and get a better handle on the projects I've actually been working on recently — the ones that should stay in the inventory because they move the needle. Getting closer to figuring that out. They center around three initiatives: **education, analytics, and research**.\n\n## 2. Signal — What surprised you, frustrated you, or changed your mind?\n\nNot surprised today. Slowly come to the realization that I take on too many projects and don't really finish anything before I attempt the next one. I need a system to prioritize my work. Because I'm largely self-directed, I'd benefit greatly from a true process to follow.\n\nAsked for help building it. Tried before, but in a better place now to build a system and a routine. The meetings with Kenny and Chris Ruf, plus the updated job description, already outline some of this. Idea: OKRs built around the primary areas I'll be evaluated on that also move the needle in the three pillars (education, analytics, research).\n\n## 3. Pattern — Connections to recent thinking?\n\nSurfaced three earlier notes on the same problem: [[self-authored-direction]] (5/22), [[the-daily-on-ramp]] (6/2), [[keep-the-main-thing-the-main-thing]] (7/31).\n\nAnswer: It does connect. Been on a long pursuit to improve productivity and figure out how to provide value. Little success so far.\n\nNot answered (carried to follow-up): what made the earlier fixes stop working, and why now is different.\n\n## 4. Essay seed — One thing you'd tell a peer about today?\n\n\"Finish one project before you start the next one.\"\n\n---\n\n## Notes produced\n- [[finish-one-project-before-you-start-the-next]]\n\n## Sources pulled\n- None. Vault notes read for Q3 only.\n\n## Open thread — follow-up session\nBuild a prioritization system with Eric: OKRs tied to (a) the areas he is evaluated on and (b) the three pillars. Inputs: `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`, `Inbox/meetings/2026-04-27_Chris_Ruf__Performance_Review__Check_ins.md`, `Inbox/meetings/2026-08-27_Applied_Performance__Job_Profiles_FMS__Logistics.md`. Open first with: why did the 5/22, 6/2 and 7/31 fixes stop working?\n######## 2026-09-09-interview.md\n---\ntype: interview\ndate: 2026-09-09\nduration_min: 10\nthemes: [ai-dependency, permission, task-lists, agentic-tooling, tech-inventory, education-initiative]\nnotes_produced: [[the-ask-has-to-precede-the-dependency]]\npulled_sources: []\n---\n\n# Interview — 2026-09-09\n\nFirst run of the two-part format. Part 0 (supervisor log) logged separately to\n`Inbox/status-log/2026-09-09.md`.\n\n## 0. Supervisor log\n\n**Anything arrive, get installed, or change hands?**\nNothing new. Following up on outstanding things only: the Joe Reed consultant (chasing\nthat down), Kenny figuring out what's going on with the Polar heart rate monitors, and\nthe FirstBeat review.\n\n**Who did you talk to that Kenny or Chris would want to know about?**\nSat in on the NCAA Sport Science Initiative (SSI) webinar about data and technology in\ncollegiate sport.\n\n**Waiting on anyone, or anything about to go sideways?**\nIT things to chase. Need to follow up with the people who owe me answers on the tech\ninventory. Need to make real progress on the Education initiative.\n\n## 1. Orientation — What did you spend time on today?\n\nOrganizing my AI agentic setup. Talked to Landon and brainstormed.\n\n## 2. Signal — What surprised you, frustrated you, or changed your mind?\n\n> \"I have this looming fear that we're going to be told we can't use AI and that's a big\n> part of what I do to get my work done these days: keep making lists to do things. I\n> just seem to continue to work to check them off.\"\n\n## 3. Pattern — Connections to recent thinking?\n\nNothing right now is connected.\n\n*(Scribe's note: two connections were found in the vault and recorded in the note rather\nthan raised in the interview — see [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]], whose central\nasymmetry this updates, and [[design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline]], which already\nholds the deeper version of the task-list seed.)*\n\n## 4. Essay seed — One thing you'd tell a peer about today?\n\n> \"Write out your tasks and then get them done.\"\n\n*(Not written up on its own. `design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline` already carries\nthe stronger form of this — the conditions are the part that never makes the list.)*\n\n---\n\n## Notes produced\n- [[the-ask-has-to-precede-the-dependency]] — acting unasked stays cheap only while a reversal stays cheap; the ask has to move before the dependency, not after it.\n\n## Notes updated\n- [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]] — Connections section extended with the limit condition.\n\n## Sources pulled\n- None.\n\n## Open threads for tomorrow\n- The OKR Q1 gate (\"re-evaluate AI integration (buy-in) + data governance\") was due\n  2026-08-31 and is still unscored. It is the ask the fear is about.\n- ~~Polar heart rate monitors: sport not identified.~~ Resolved same session:\n  **Women's Basketball**. Log updated.\n- ~~Landon conversation not recorded in the supervisor log.~~ Resolved same session:\n  added to the log's People & conversations.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"18a72a79-ff33-4a51-897e-51ccc4522b0b","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:22:58.232Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"######## meetings/2026-09-09_NCAA_SSI_Webinar__Performance_Tech__Data_Governance.md\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n\n# NCAA SSI Webinar — Performance Tech & Data Governance\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-09 11:00\n**Duration:** 45m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Responsible Technology Use:** Balance innovation with athlete health; set policies, educate, secure data, and regularly review technology impact.\n\n- **Technology as a Tool:** Use wearables and sensors to guide decisions, not replace context-aware judgment; avoid data overload and misuse.\n\n- **Data Privacy and Security:** Consult legal teams on data laws; ensure secure storage and clear athlete consent; watch for vendor data selling.\n\n- **Stakeholder Education:** Train staff deeply on data meaning; keep coaches informed without overwhelming; fully educate athletes and obtain consent.\n\n- **Technology Limitations:** No single metric reliably predicts performance; be wary of opaque algorithms; consider mental health effects of data use.\n\n- **Strategic Integration:** Link medical and performance data for injury prevention; continuously evaluate tech value and foster multidisciplinary collaboration.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Tim Coffey**\nEngage with student athletes to gather feedback on their experiences and perceptions regarding performance technologies and data use (38:18)\nEncourage continuous education of performance and sports medicine staff on validity, reliability, and interpretation of performance technology data (21:46)\n\n**Mark Koberly**\nDevelop and maintain a written plan/policy framework for performance technology adoption including justification, validity checks, data privacy, and access protocols (13:37)\nCoordinate with institutional general counsel, IT, and risk management to ascertain legal and security compliance in data collection, storage, and use (31:06)\nImplement routine reevaluations of performance technology effectiveness based on actionable outcomes and stakeholder feedback (17:22)\n\n**Tim Coffey and Mark Koberly**\nPromote multidisciplinary stakeholder education including coaches and student athletes before deploying new technology (21:46)\nAdvocate for transparency and consent regarding data collection and use with student athletes to mitigate anxiety and privacy concerns (20:16)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nperformance technology, student athlete data, data privacy, educational programs, injury prevention, policy development\n\n######## meetings/2026-09-10_Sep_10_0937_AM.md\n## Project tags\n> Auto-tagged from `data/meeting-topics.json`. Edit freely - add what was missed, delete what is wrong. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n# Sep 10, 09:37 AM\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-10 09:40\n**Duration:** 51m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Athlete Health Priority:** Immediate medical care given during athlete distress; heat acclimation stressed for matches with temps near 97°F.\n\n- **Training Adjustments:** New schedules with lifting days, rest days, and veteran leadership to integrate transfers and manage larger rosters.\n\n- **Equipment Upgrades:** Approved purchase of Kaiser leg press A400 model for advanced metrics; garage door repaired after equipment storage damage.\n\n- **Data Use Caution:** Emphasis on balanced tech use, athlete consent, and education to prevent data overload and mental stress.\n\n- **Staff Changes:** Kevin Gall left, duties shifted; new consultant Joe seeks medical record access with privacy concerns noted.\n\n- **Competition Updates:** Cross country finished 3rd–4th; equestrian meet and track championships upcoming; volleyball and softball easing into season.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Unassigned**\nCommunicate and coordinate athlete seating and lifting logistics with coach for seasonal starts and Sunday match (00:00)\nMonitor athlete with prosthetic leg carefully and avoid unnecessary training modifications unless medically indicated (02:16)\nPrepare cross-country athletes for next meet at Texas A&M and continue with regular/modified training cards (03:43)\nImplement impact tracking pilot with five tumblers and collect data for volume insights (07:19)\nContinue managing increased equestrian roster and prepare for first meet against South Dakota at 11am (08:48)\nManage sprinters and hurdlers practicing independently during coaching absence, focusing on heat safety (13:10)\nManage softball and volleyball post-county fair training and schedule; address health concerns and equipment issues (17:31)\nReinforce equipment storage protocols to avoid damage and secure cords and dumbbells against theft (22:27)\nMaintain awareness of mental health resources and schedule breaks; consider participating in 911 stair climb fundraiser (24:14)\nIntegrate learnings from NCAA webinar on technology use and educate athletes about data privacy, accuracy, and mental health impact (26:06)\nEducate athletes using wearable tech on limitations, especially regarding sleep and heart rate variability data to reduce negative psychological effects (31:47)\nPrepare for installation of new Kaiser leg press, considering location logistics and evaluating cost benefit of advanced model (36:05)\nRSVP to upcoming Chipotle tailgate event as needed (41:42)\nRedistribute responsibilities for staff roles formerly managed by Kevin Gall, mainly among Cali and Kendall (43:24)\nCoordinate with external rehab provider Joe regarding data sharing and medical record integration to avoid duplicate documentation (49:19)\nFinalize soccer team travel and match attendance schedule, managing upcoming games and trips (50:47)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nathlete management, training adaptations, wearable technology, injury prevention, team health incidents, equipment maintenance\n\n\n######## meetings/2026-09-14_Team_Sync__Hiring_Process_ITS__TB_Updates.md\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n# Team Sync — Hiring Process, ITS & TB Updates\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-14 11:30\n**Duration:** 42m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Hiring Process:** Structured checkpoints enhance quality hires; leadership roles need strong mission alignment; student employee shifts require formal approvals.  \n- **Event Experience:** New lighting, fireworks improve fan atmosphere; faith-based elements well received but need better player prep; staff support boosts team culture.  \n- **Student Survey Engagement:** Only 7 of 500 athletes responded; anonymity assured; next surveys target gratitude and services; Teamworks cleanup underway with 4,000+ profiles.  \n- **IT Support Issues:** Slow IT response frustrates staff; admin password removal hampers software installs; single contact and escalation path set to improve outcomes.  \n- **Health & Safety:** Two athletes tested as TB close contacts but symptom-free; staff member’s child tested positive, all cleared; monitoring and routine screening active.  \n- **Communication Tools:** Microsoft Teams promoted for updates; website improvements planned with multimedia content; campus partnerships tracked for reports and visibility.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Unassigned**\nHolly to return to office later this week to assist with operations (00:25)\nHiring committee to continue interviewing and assessing candidates for associate director of NIL education (00:25)\nStaff to provide feedback on bank service complaints, especially regarding football players’ use of to-go boxes (04:10)\nCoaches and liaisons to monitor and elevate academic concerns from initial grade reports (05:15)\nContinue promoting mental health check-ins with student athletes to improve completion rates (06:00)\nSupervisors to conduct one-on-one performance reviews with direct reports before December scheduling conflicts arise, with proper documentation (21:45)\nAll staff encouraged to actively use Microsoft Teams channel for internal communications about events, deadlines, and student athlete engagement (22:24)\nCoordinate with Cody Soto to update multimedia content for the Baylor Athletics website (23:45)\nResolve administrative password access issues to enable efficient software use on staff laptops (33:45)\nFinalize and circulate NCAA attestation report draft for review by end of September; ensure timely submission by mid-October (36:40)\nDocument and track campus collaboration efforts for reporting purposes (37:50)\nInform and provide support for athletes concerning TB testing protocols; disseminate symptom information to appropriate staff (39:10)\nDistribute Parents Weekend schedule and details to relevant personnel (41:50)\n\n**Josh Barnhill**\nStaff to meet with new baseball trainer and athletic case manager as part of orientation (06:00)\n\n**Hiring Managers, Direct Reports**\nHiring managers to adhere to outlined personnel hiring process: initial planning, mid-cycle check-ins, finalist plan, and pre-offer communication with leadership (14:16)\n\n**Malcolm and others**\nTeam leaders to promote Real Response survey participation using QR codes and in meetings (25:45)\n\n**Eric Rash**\nEric Rash to continue managing communications with ITS and escalate unresolved issues as needed; Jovan to copy leadership on ongoing ITS issues (30:40)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nHiring process, Mental health check-ins, NCAA attestation, TB testing, Student athlete engagement, ITS communication\n\n######## status-log/2026-09-14.md\n---\ndate: 2026-09-14\nsports: [Volleyball, Men's Tennis, Football]\n---\n\n## Equipment & installs\n- Nothing changed hands or was installed.\n\n## People & conversations\n- Joe Reed (consultant, Men's Tennis) — met with him last week.\n- Football — helped collect jumps today.\n- Athletic training staff — made the rounds.\n- Student services staff meeting — attended.\n- Micah Lamb — sent a follow-up email about the tech review process.\n- Tech inventory — got the laggard departments to fill it out. Volleyball is the only one outstanding.\n\n## Waiting on / risks\n- Micah Lamb — no response on the tech review process, about 4 weeks now.\n- Waiting on ITS to reply about our tech approval framework.\n- Going to meet with Toby Brooks to help build out the education curriculum for Applied Performance.\n######## status-log/2026-09-09.md\n---\ndate: 2026-09-09\nsports: [Women's Basketball, Acrobatics & Tumbling]\n---\n\n## Equipment & installs\n- Nothing new arrived or got installed. Follow-up work only:\n  - Joe Reed consultant — chasing that down.\n  - Kenny figuring out what's going on with the Polar heart rate monitors (Women's Basketball).\n  - FirstBeat review.\n\n## People & conversations\n- Sat in on the NCAA Sport Science Initiative (SSI) webinar about data and technology in collegiate sport.\n- Landon — brainstormed while organizing my AI agentic setup.\n- Acro & Tumbling staff (meeting was 09-08, captured here on 09-09 from the transcript) — planning an impact-counting system for tumbling load. Five-athlete pilot, manual tracking to start, wearables later once budget allows. **I own the build:** an Excel \"pass builder\" that maps tumbling routines to cumulative impact load. Waiting on them for the skill inventory with per-skill hand/foot impact counts, the daily practice assignments, and a consistent skill naming convention.\n  Source: `Inbox/meetings/2026-09-08_Acro__Tumbling__Impact_Load_Tracking_Pilot.md`\n\n## Waiting on / risks\n- IT things to chase down.\n- Tech inventory — waiting on the people who need to get back to me.\n- Education initiative needs real progress.\n######## 2026-09-14-interview.md\n---\ntype: interview\ndate: 2026-09-14\nduration_min: 15\nthemes: [prioritization, finishing-work, OKRs, three-pillars, productivity-system]\nnotes_produced: [\"[[finish-one-project-before-you-start-the-next]]\"]\npulled_sources: []\n---\n\n# Interview — 2026-09-14\n\nSupervisor log for today: `Inbox/status-log/2026-09-14.md`\n\n## 1. Orientation — What did you spend time on today?\n\nHelped football collect jumps, then made the rounds with the athletic training staff. Went to a student services staff meeting. Then tried to map out and get a better handle on the projects I've actually been working on recently — the ones that should stay in the inventory because they move the needle. Getting closer to figuring that out. They center around three initiatives: **education, analytics, and research**.\n\n## 2. Signal — What surprised you, frustrated you, or changed your mind?\n\nNot surprised today. Slowly come to the realization that I take on too many projects and don't really finish anything before I attempt the next one. I need a system to prioritize my work. Because I'm largely self-directed, I'd benefit greatly from a true process to follow.\n\nAsked for help building it. Tried before, but in a better place now to build a system and a routine. The meetings with Kenny and Chris Ruf, plus the updated job description, already outline some of this. Idea: OKRs built around the primary areas I'll be evaluated on that also move the needle in the three pillars (education, analytics, research).\n\n## 3. Pattern — Connections to recent thinking?\n\nSurfaced three earlier notes on the same problem: [[self-authored-direction]] (5/22), [[the-daily-on-ramp]] (6/2), [[keep-the-main-thing-the-main-thing]] (7/31).\n\nAnswer: It does connect. Been on a long pursuit to improve productivity and figure out how to provide value. Little success so far.\n\nNot answered (carried to follow-up): what made the earlier fixes stop working, and why now is different.\n\n## 4. Essay seed — One thing you'd tell a peer about today?\n\n\"Finish one project before you start the next one.\"\n\n---\n\n## Notes produced\n- [[finish-one-project-before-you-start-the-next]]\n\n## Sources pulled\n- None. Vault notes read for Q3 only.\n\n## Open thread — follow-up session\nBuild a prioritization system with Eric: OKRs tied to (a) the areas he is evaluated on and (b) the three pillars. Inputs: `Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026/`, `Inbox/meetings/2026-04-27_Chris_Ruf__Performance_Review__Check_ins.md`, `Inbox/meetings/2026-08-27_Applied_Performance__Job_Profiles_FMS__Logistics.md`. Open first with: why did the 5/22, 6/2 and 7/31 fixes stop working?\n######## 2026-09-09-interview.md\n---\ntype: interview\ndate: 2026-09-09\nduration_min: 10\nthemes: [ai-dependency, permission, task-lists, agentic-tooling, tech-inventory, education-initiative]\nnotes_produced: [[the-ask-has-to-precede-the-dependency]]\npulled_sources: []\n---\n\n# Interview — 2026-09-09\n\nFirst run of the two-part format. Part 0 (supervisor log) logged separately to\n`Inbox/status-log/2026-09-09.md`.\n\n## 0. Supervisor log\n\n**Anything arrive, get installed, or change hands?**\nNothing new. Following up on outstanding things only: the Joe Reed consultant (chasing\nthat down), Kenny figuring out what's going on with the Polar heart rate monitors, and\nthe FirstBeat review.\n\n**Who did you talk to that Kenny or Chris would want to know about?**\nSat in on the NCAA Sport Science Initiative (SSI) webinar about data and technology in\ncollegiate sport.\n\n**Waiting on anyone, or anything about to go sideways?**\nIT things to chase. Need to follow up with the people who owe me answers on the tech\ninventory. Need to make real progress on the Education initiative.\n\n## 1. Orientation — What did you spend time on today?\n\nOrganizing my AI agentic setup. Talked to Landon and brainstormed.\n\n## 2. Signal — What surprised you, frustrated you, or changed your mind?\n\n> \"I have this looming fear that we're going to be told we can't use AI and that's a big\n> part of what I do to get my work done these days: keep making lists to do things. I\n> just seem to continue to work to check them off.\"\n\n## 3. Pattern — Connections to recent thinking?\n\nNothing right now is connected.\n\n*(Scribe's note: two connections were found in the vault and recorded in the note rather\nthan raised in the interview — see [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]], whose central\nasymmetry this updates, and [[design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline]], which already\nholds the deeper version of the task-list seed.)*\n\n## 4. Essay seed — One thing you'd tell a peer about today?\n\n> \"Write out your tasks and then get them done.\"\n\n*(Not written up on its own. `design-the-conditions-not-the-discipline` already carries\nthe stronger form of this — the conditions are the part that never makes the list.)*\n\n---\n\n## Notes produced\n- [[the-ask-has-to-precede-the-dependency]] — acting unasked stays cheap only while a reversal stays cheap; the ask has to move before the dependency, not after it.\n\n## Notes updated\n- [[waiting-for-the-ask-kills-the-idea]] — Connections section extended with the limit condition.\n\n## Sources pulled\n- None.\n\n## Open threads for tomorrow\n- The OKR Q1 gate (\"re-evaluate AI integration (buy-in) + data governance\") was due\n  2026-08-31 and is still unscored. It is the ask the fear is about.\n- ~~Polar heart rate monitors: sport not identified.~~ Resolved same session:\n  **Women's Basketball**. Log updated.\n- ~~Landon conversation not recorded in the supervisor log.~~ Resolved same session:\n  added to the log's People & conversations.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"be1df91c-e850-4ee7-b509-27f649f622ed","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"18a72a79-ff33-4a51-897e-51ccc4522b0b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf4MFzeJdGoWT9PC7GHZY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mi8cXkXWmvhYfWaBUeozCp","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/SecondBrain/Inbox && for f in meetings/2026-09-02_Applied_Performance__Customer_Discovery__Content_Plan.md meetings/2026-09-03_Joe_CLA_CourseDesign.md meetings/2026-08-24_Athletics_All_Staff__Personnel__Software_Inventory.md; do echo \"######## $f\"; cat \"$f\"; done; echo \"######## OKR\"; cat ~/SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-OKRs-2026-27.md","description":"Read curriculum, Joe CLA, all-staff notes and OKR doc"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15266,"cache_read_input_tokens":77989,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15266,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Mi8cXkXWmvhYfWaBUeozCp":{"command":"cd ~/SecondBrain/Inbox && for f in meetings/2026-09-02_Applied_Performance__Customer_Discovery__Content_Plan.md meetings/2026-09-03_Joe_CLA_CourseDesign.md meetings/2026-08-24_Athletics_All_Staff__Personnel__Software_Inventory.md; do echo \"######## $f\"; cat \"$f\"; done; echo \"######## OKR\"; cat ~/SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-OKRs-2026-27.md","description":"Read curriculum, Joe CLA, all-staff notes and OKR doc"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cf4MFymTkxC6dr157UTA4","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionMcpServer":"plugin:context-mode:context-mode","attributionMcpTool":"ctx_execute","type":"assistant","uuid":"b38f3328-3c89-4bab-ad51-9fa5f71c1d19","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:22:59.500Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b38f3328-3c89-4bab-ad51-9fa5f71c1d19","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mi8cXkXWmvhYfWaBUeozCp","type":"tool_result","content":"######## meetings/2026-09-02_Applied_Performance__Customer_Discovery__Content_Plan.md\n# Applied Performance — Customer Discovery & Content Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-02 09:45\n**Duration:** 72m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Applied Performance Role:** Focus on service, education, and adaptable resources over direct athlete interaction; includes research and analytics duties.  \n- **Data System Development:** Students prep data; goal to deliver quick proof of concepts; open to external expertise for scaling.  \n- **Customer Discovery:** Semi-structured interviews with strength coaches; diverse sports sample; use AI tools for fast transcription and analysis.  \n- **Content Strategy:** Two tracks—short clips for athletes, deeper sessions for staff; AI tools automate video editing and posting.  \n- **Process & Deadlines:** Produce four videos by September end; follow with AI-driven data analysis and more content releases by November.  \n- **Market Insights:** Technology gaps hinder accurate data; coaches frustrated; cultural buy-in and leadership support critical for adoption.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Eric Rash**\nDevelop initial customer discovery interview questions tailored for strength coaches and athletic staff by early September (29:19)\nSchedule and conduct interviews with a representative sample (~80%) of strength coaches focusing on data needs, readiness questions, and cultural support for applied performance (18:36)\nCollaborate on creating educational content in two tracks: short actionable pieces for athletes and detailed technical sessions for coaches and staff (35:47)\nPilot content creation using Riverside or similar tool, including video recording, AI transcription, and clipping, with assistance from student assistants (38:15)\nCoordinate with internal stakeholders (athletic medicine, coaches, administrators) to understand cultural and budgetary constraints affecting adoption of applied performance initiatives (29:19)\nBuild out mockup applied performance website and newsletter to serve as content hub (01:06:15)\nPlan and adhere to a timeline: complete interview phase by late September, analyze data with AI tools, then push out four videos by mid-October to early November (01:07:53)\nContinue education and culture development initiatives beyond preceptorship role to sustain long-term applied performance integration (01:09:28)\nAttend follow-up meetings scheduled tentatively for Fridays or midweek after 5 pm to maintain project momentum and accountability (01:11:30)\n\n**Meeting Organizer/Unknown Speaker**\nAssist Eric Rash in refining interview scripts based on initial feedback and provide expertise on interview methodology and qualitative data analysis (19:53)\nProvide guidance on AI tools for transcription and data synthesis, like Fireflies and Granola, and demonstrate video production workflow using Riverside (38:15)\nSupport content strategy development focusing on actionable, bite-sized educational content and framework for measuring success (58:00)\nShare insights from past educational programs (Performance School/Academy) and recommend delivery formats to increase engagement (01:02:30)\nCollaborate on setting up Canvas course infrastructure and safeguarding content access for internal distribution with optional assignments and quizzes (01:04:30)\nCommit to meeting and interview scheduling support; offer to sit in on interviews and advise on execution plan and accountability (01:07:53)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nApplied Performance, Customer Discovery, Educational Content, Data-Driven Metrics, Strength Coaches, AI Tools\n\n######## meetings/2026-09-03_Joe_CLA_CourseDesign.md\n# Joe_CLA_CourseDesign\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-03 14:10\n**Duration:** 47m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Return to Performance Focus:** Aims to rebuild full athlete capability beyond clearance, covering biomechanics, conditioning, and mental readiness.  \n- **Session Design:** Uses needs assessment and backward planning from game dates, adjusting progress based on subjective and objective feedback.  \n- **Collaboration & Gatekeeping:** Director of Rehab and Head Trainer approve plans; strong communication needed to manage skepticism from less familiar staff.  \n- **Learning Commitment:** Requires 3-4 hours weekly for theory and practice; coaching and constructive feedback are essential for skill growth.  \n- **Technology & Data Integration:** Uses GPS, force plates, video, and subjective athlete scores to create tailored, defensible session plans with performance monitoring.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Unassigned**\nProvide equipment, such as a tripod, to enable video recording of sessions for later review and objective data collection (32:33)\nDevelop a session builder tool incorporating scientifically validated principles, including reverse calendar progression and drill checklists, to aid in planning individualized return to performance sessions (28:00)\nEducate staff on ecological dynamics and constraints-led theory to deepen understanding and application in return to performance programming (14:21)\nEnsure ongoing documentation of session notes capturing drills performed and athlete feedback to maintain comprehensive records alongside the session builder tool (28:00)\nMonitor staff changes and potential implications on program buy-in and trust, especially with strength and conditioning personnel, to maintain continuity and support for the return to performance role (22:11)\nContinue capturing subjective and objective data to support return to performance decisions, including collaboration with rehab and strength & conditioning staff (17:22)\n\n**Jacob (Director of Rehab)**\nOversee and approve the return to performance protocols and certify athlete readiness for progression (24:30)\n\n**Tim (Head Athletic Trainer)**\nCollaborate on integrating training and rehab programs; contribute to clearance decisions and session reviews (24:30)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nreturn to performance, athletic training, ecological dynamics, constraints-led approach, rehab progression, session design\n\n######## meetings/2026-08-24_Athletics_All_Staff__Personnel__Software_Inventory.md\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n\n# Athletics All-Staff — Personnel & Software Inventory\n\n**Date:** 2026-08-24 11:35\n**Duration:** 42m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Event Execution and Engagement:** Tailgate boosted athlete camaraderie despite heat and gate confusion; future events need clearer communication and possibly extended education sessions.  \n- **Personnel Updates and Onboarding:** Two new hires start in September; virtual interviews ongoing for several positions from 300 applicants; personnel folder improvements underway.  \n- **Technology and Communication Systems:** Teamworks use is being pushed for compliance and scheduling; software inventory and ITS communication streamlined; email etiquette reminders issued.  \n- **Compliance and Athlete Status Management:** Litigation-affected athletes managed carefully; eligibility and roster status being clarified; compliance experts are main contacts for questions.  \n- **Strategic Leadership and Department Culture:** Leaders promote unity, presence, and empathy; stress breaking silos and being active with staff and athletes during transitions.  \n- **Upcoming Meetings and Follow-Ups:** Unit leaders to update annual report status; mandatory student employee meeting Thursday; next department meeting skipped due to leadership absence.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Kevin Robinson**\nKevin Robinson to continue filling in for baseball until Aaron Burgagen starts on Sept 8 (10:00)\n\n**Shelley Doyle**\nShelley Doyle to complete background check process to officially start athletics case manager position Sept 21 (11:04)\n\n**Mike**\nMike to complete virtual interviews for open position by next Monday (12:45)\n\n**Unit Leads and Tyler**\nUnit leads to check attendance records post-meeting and follow up with absent staff; coordinate with Tyler on makeup session (08:50)\n\n**Unassigned**\nInformation and executive summary on Teamworks roles to be sent out to all staff for increased usage and cleanup of inactive accounts (22:03)\n\n**Eric Rash**\nEric to inventory all departmental software and coordinate increased ITS communication via weekly meetings with Michael Lamb (23:37)\n\n**Unit Leaders**\nUnit leaders to assign staff to complete survey by soft deadline Sept 29 and ensure prompt submission (26:45)\n\n**Tyler**\nTyler to assist in providing summary status on NCAA 5-for-5 athletes in litigation for sports-specific compliance follow-up (30:30)\n\n**All Staff**\nAll staff to include department lead in donor/fundraising conversations and ensure communication transparency (33:29)\nStaff to manage email communication carefully to reduce misunderstandings, favor calls or short messages for sensitive topics (34:57)\nAll to maintain visible presence at practices, events, and for student-athlete interactions; communicate absences clearly (37:55)\nMeeting attendance next week canceled; prepare for resumed meetings following week (41:44)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nathletic personnel, tailgate event, Teamworks platform, NCAA litigation, fundraising communication, software inventory\n\n######## OKR\n---\ntype: strategy\nproject: Applied Performance — Unit OKRs\ncreated: 2026-05-29\nowner: Eric Rash\nunit_members: [Eric Rash, AJ (AP Specialist — Football), Noah (BB AP GA)]\ncycle: Semester cycles aligned to athletic calendar — Summer close-out (Jun–Aug 2026), Fall (Sep–Dec 2026), Spring (Jan–May 2027)\ncurrent_cycle: Summer close-out (through 2026-08-31)\nnext_review: 2026-08-31 (cycle close + Fall set authored); mid-semester checkpoints ~Oct 15 and ~Mar 1\nstatus: v3 — restructured 2026-07-17 around the new responsibilities document (JD) and Jul 9 planning meeting; decision records in [[Applied-Performance-JD-2026/decisions]]\n---\n\n# Applied Performance — Unit OKRs (2026–27)\n\n## Vision (North Star)\n**Be the best applied performance department in the country.**\n\nThat's the bar. A KR is \"done\" only at a level a top-5 program would recognize, not just \"we did a thing.\"\n\n## Mission (how we get there)\nTurn Baylor's athlete data into decisions that keep athletes available and performing, build the data and performance literacy of the people who use it, and produce original research that advances both the program and the university's R1 mission.\n\n## Structure (v3)\nThree permanent **service pillars** — Data Systems & Analytics, Education, Research & Development — plus one **enabling objective** (Leadership & Visibility) that the new responsibilities document made explicit: the unit now delivers through people and processes Eric directs, not only through work Eric performs. Role-language definitions (Oversee, Assist, Support-and-guide, Unified) live in [[Applied-Performance-JD-2026/CONTEXT]].\n\n**Cycle mechanics:** KRs are committed per semester (quarterly rhythm retired 2026-07-17 — decision 0007). Every KR is scored at the mid-semester checkpoint (on-track/off-track) and at cycle close (0.0–1.0, keep/retire/add). The biweekly Friday report to Chris is the standing pulse between checkpoints. Every KR must have its critical path inside the unit's control — no KR whose completion depends on an external party's timeline.\n\n**Eric's protected hands-on lanes** (not delegated, not reviewed-only — built by his hands): HPI protocol and its dashboarding; technology vetting; white-paper analysis; one-off report and dashboard creation for sports. Delegation stops at these boundaries.\n\n---\n\n## Objective 1 — Data Systems & Infrastructure\n> **The data layer is standardized, governed, and survives staff transition; sport-facing delivery is bespoke on top of it and routinely drives coaching decisions.**\n\n*\"Unified\" means standardized underneath (metric definitions, data model, freshness), bespoke on top (per-sport dashboards and reports). A single all-programs dashboard is explicitly not the goal (decision 0008).*\n\n**Summer close-out KRs (through Aug 31)**\n| # | Key result | Owner | Target | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.1 | **Per-sport coach dashboards in routine weekly use** — Football (delivered through AJ) + Volleyball (direct) — built on shared data standards. *\"Used\" = referenced in the team's weekly planning meeting; core metrics fresh same-day.* | AJ (FB), Eric (VB) | ≥5 of 6 weeks, Jul 15 – Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n| 1.2 | **Technology decision matrix revived and adopted as standard vendor intake** — documented + Chris sign-off in writing. No named vendor; the next real pitch flows through it. | Eric | Adopted by Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n| 1.3 | **HPI protocol v1 drafted** — data integration, flow, storage, dashboarding, interpretation — circulated to Chris + Kenny for feedback; walked through with Kenny in a meeting Eric schedules. | Eric | Circulated by Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n\n**Staged to Fall:** data-continuity plan per NCAA Best Practices document (what survives a staff transition — informed by the Blake departure); website-era distribution architecture; Kitman/data-management system improvements.\n\n## Objective 2 — Education\n> **Applied Performance is a recognized source of education in the building — a launched, running engine, not an aspirational one.**\n\n*Reset 2026-07-17 (decision 0006): the original 6-issue target was 0-for-3 at mid-July. The blocker was launch — an open-ended sign-off loop and distribution re-planning around the unlaunched website. New rules: advance-notice, not approval-seeking (\"shipping on X unless you want changes\"); email distribution now, website as the Fall upgrade.*\n\n**Summer close-out KRs (through Aug 31)**\n| # | Key result | Owner | Target | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2.1 | **Engine launched — 2 issues shipped**: Issue #1 ~Aug 1 anchored by the sleep education module; Issue #2 ~Aug 22 anchored by the GLP-1/performance brief. Proof-of-concept to Chris + stakeholders the week before Issue #1 with a stated ship date. | Eric | 2 issues by Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n| 2.2 | **Both leadership-requested modules circulated** — sleep education + GLP-1 brief in named stakeholders' hands for feedback (the initiative signal the Jul 9 meeting asked for). | Eric | Circulated by Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n| 2.3 | **Reach + demand baseline** — distribution counts recorded at each issue; inbound requests tracked. Targets set from evidence at the Aug 31 review, not guessed now. | Eric | Baseline recorded | ⬜ |\n\n**Staged to Fall:** cadence set from evidence (monthly floor); website distribution; staff/athlete live-session targets; AP Clinic contributions as directed by Courteney (she owns the event — Eric assists).\n\n## Objective 3 — Research & Development\n> **A real R&D engine — committee, repository, formalized partners — that produces publishable output and visibly feeds Baylor's R1 strategy.**\n\n**Summer close-out KRs (through Aug 31)**\n| # | Key result | Owner | Target | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3.1 | **BAIR Steering Committee organized** — vetting process documented, first meeting scheduled with agenda. | Eric | By Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n| 3.2 | **Research repository MVP live** — catalog of projects, status, data, partners. | Eric | By Jul 31 (score honestly at review if late) | ⬜ |\n| 3.3 | **≥1 academic partnership formalized** — named project, written scope + timeline. | Eric | By Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n| 3.4 | **White paper drafted** — converted from the existing short report. Eric sole owner of the critical path; Blake credited as co-author, zero dependency on post-departure contributions (decision 0005). Publication deployment = Fall. | Eric | Drafted by Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n\n## Objective 4 — Leadership & Visibility (enabling)\n> **The director model demonstrably works: staff own their lanes, leadership is never surprised, and the department's work is visible without being asked for.**\n\n**Summer close-out KRs (through Aug 31)**\n| # | Key result | Owner | Target | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4.1 | **Friday report cadence live** — biweekly email to Chris: Shipped / In Progress / Decisions Needed / Risks–No-Surprises / Next Two Weeks, <300 words. The decision-impact log feeds \"Shipped\" (it is no longer a standalone KR — it's ammunition). | Eric | ≥3 sent by Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n| 4.2 | **AJ full handoff through fall camp** — AJ owns camp planning and the plan-vs-actual review to the football staff (framework already jointly presented); Eric reviews all artifacts pre-delivery, weekly 1:1 standing (decision 0004). | AJ, Eric oversight | 100% of camp practices on framework; review delivered ≤1 wk post-camp | ⬜ |\n| 4.3 | **Noah mentoring cadence** — onboarding checklist written; weekly 1:1; departmental policies/procedures walked through. | Eric | Checklist + cadence live by Aug 15 | ⬜ |\n| 4.4 | **Proactive supervisor meetings held** — Kenny (HPI/research walkthrough) + H&W technology guidance, both initiated by Eric. | Eric | Both held by Aug 31 | ⬜ |\n\n---\n\n## Aug 31 review (cycle close)\nScore each KR 0.0–1.0, keep/retire/add, then author the **Fall (Sep–Dec) set** — first semester cycle, mid-semester checkpoint ~Oct 15. Set education reach targets and newsletter cadence from actuals. Fall candidates already staged: continuity plan, white-paper publication, website distribution, Linda's role integration (pending the Chris conversation), data-management system build.\n\n## Parking lot\n- White paper **published** — Fall.\n- Peer-reviewed submission — the longer-horizon outcome behind the white papers.\n- Year-end research summary — Spring close.\n- Website-based education hub (with Cody) — Fall distribution upgrade.\n\n## Superseded (v2 → v3, 2026-07-17)\n- ~~Unified single dashboard across programs~~ → per-sport on shared data standards (0008).\n- ~~6 newsletter issues / twice-monthly~~ → launch-first reset, 2 module-anchored issues (0006).\n- ~~Quarterly KR cycles~~ → semester cycles + mid-semester checkpoints (0007).\n- ~~Eric delivers fall-camp framework + Aranda review~~ → AJ owns, Eric oversees (0004).\n- ~~Decision-impact log as KR 1.3~~ → fuel for the Friday report (not scored separately).\n- ~~Blake as unit member / KR co-owner~~ → departed; ownership redistributed (0003).","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"f33d97a6-e20b-4383-be70-68e1fe6c56e9","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:23:00.759Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"######## meetings/2026-09-02_Applied_Performance__Customer_Discovery__Content_Plan.md\n# Applied Performance — Customer Discovery & Content Plan\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-02 09:45\n**Duration:** 72m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Applied Performance Role:** Focus on service, education, and adaptable resources over direct athlete interaction; includes research and analytics duties.  \n- **Data System Development:** Students prep data; goal to deliver quick proof of concepts; open to external expertise for scaling.  \n- **Customer Discovery:** Semi-structured interviews with strength coaches; diverse sports sample; use AI tools for fast transcription and analysis.  \n- **Content Strategy:** Two tracks—short clips for athletes, deeper sessions for staff; AI tools automate video editing and posting.  \n- **Process & Deadlines:** Produce four videos by September end; follow with AI-driven data analysis and more content releases by November.  \n- **Market Insights:** Technology gaps hinder accurate data; coaches frustrated; cultural buy-in and leadership support critical for adoption.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Eric Rash**\nDevelop initial customer discovery interview questions tailored for strength coaches and athletic staff by early September (29:19)\nSchedule and conduct interviews with a representative sample (~80%) of strength coaches focusing on data needs, readiness questions, and cultural support for applied performance (18:36)\nCollaborate on creating educational content in two tracks: short actionable pieces for athletes and detailed technical sessions for coaches and staff (35:47)\nPilot content creation using Riverside or similar tool, including video recording, AI transcription, and clipping, with assistance from student assistants (38:15)\nCoordinate with internal stakeholders (athletic medicine, coaches, administrators) to understand cultural and budgetary constraints affecting adoption of applied performance initiatives (29:19)\nBuild out mockup applied performance website and newsletter to serve as content hub (01:06:15)\nPlan and adhere to a timeline: complete interview phase by late September, analyze data with AI tools, then push out four videos by mid-October to early November (01:07:53)\nContinue education and culture development initiatives beyond preceptorship role to sustain long-term applied performance integration (01:09:28)\nAttend follow-up meetings scheduled tentatively for Fridays or midweek after 5 pm to maintain project momentum and accountability (01:11:30)\n\n**Meeting Organizer/Unknown Speaker**\nAssist Eric Rash in refining interview scripts based on initial feedback and provide expertise on interview methodology and qualitative data analysis (19:53)\nProvide guidance on AI tools for transcription and data synthesis, like Fireflies and Granola, and demonstrate video production workflow using Riverside (38:15)\nSupport content strategy development focusing on actionable, bite-sized educational content and framework for measuring success (58:00)\nShare insights from past educational programs (Performance School/Academy) and recommend delivery formats to increase engagement (01:02:30)\nCollaborate on setting up Canvas course infrastructure and safeguarding content access for internal distribution with optional assignments and quizzes (01:04:30)\nCommit to meeting and interview scheduling support; offer to sit in on interviews and advise on execution plan and accountability (01:07:53)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nApplied Performance, Customer Discovery, Educational Content, Data-Driven Metrics, Strength Coaches, AI Tools\n\n######## meetings/2026-09-03_Joe_CLA_CourseDesign.md\n# Joe_CLA_CourseDesign\n\n**Date:** 2026-09-03 14:10\n**Duration:** 47m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Return to Performance Focus:** Aims to rebuild full athlete capability beyond clearance, covering biomechanics, conditioning, and mental readiness.  \n- **Session Design:** Uses needs assessment and backward planning from game dates, adjusting progress based on subjective and objective feedback.  \n- **Collaboration & Gatekeeping:** Director of Rehab and Head Trainer approve plans; strong communication needed to manage skepticism from less familiar staff.  \n- **Learning Commitment:** Requires 3-4 hours weekly for theory and practice; coaching and constructive feedback are essential for skill growth.  \n- **Technology & Data Integration:** Uses GPS, force plates, video, and subjective athlete scores to create tailored, defensible session plans with performance monitoring.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Unassigned**\nProvide equipment, such as a tripod, to enable video recording of sessions for later review and objective data collection (32:33)\nDevelop a session builder tool incorporating scientifically validated principles, including reverse calendar progression and drill checklists, to aid in planning individualized return to performance sessions (28:00)\nEducate staff on ecological dynamics and constraints-led theory to deepen understanding and application in return to performance programming (14:21)\nEnsure ongoing documentation of session notes capturing drills performed and athlete feedback to maintain comprehensive records alongside the session builder tool (28:00)\nMonitor staff changes and potential implications on program buy-in and trust, especially with strength and conditioning personnel, to maintain continuity and support for the return to performance role (22:11)\nContinue capturing subjective and objective data to support return to performance decisions, including collaboration with rehab and strength & conditioning staff (17:22)\n\n**Jacob (Director of Rehab)**\nOversee and approve the return to performance protocols and certify athlete readiness for progression (24:30)\n\n**Tim (Head Athletic Trainer)**\nCollaborate on integrating training and rehab programs; contribute to clearance decisions and session reviews (24:30)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nreturn to performance, athletic training, ecological dynamics, constraints-led approach, rehab progression, session design\n\n######## meetings/2026-08-24_Athletics_All_Staff__Personnel__Software_Inventory.md\n## Project tags\n> Tag this meeting against active projects. Add freely. Used by weekly-review to surface drift.\n\n- project/\n- person/\n\n## Key topics\n\n## Decisions\n\n## Action items\n> Pulled from Fireflies. Mark complete by replacing `[ ]` with `[x]`.\n\n## Open questions\n\n# Athletics All-Staff — Personnel & Software Inventory\n\n**Date:** 2026-08-24 11:35\n**Duration:** 42m\n**Status:** PROCESSED\n**Organizer:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n**Participants:** eric.rash11@gmail.com\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Event Execution and Engagement:** Tailgate boosted athlete camaraderie despite heat and gate confusion; future events need clearer communication and possibly extended education sessions.  \n- **Personnel Updates and Onboarding:** Two new hires start in September; virtual interviews ongoing for several positions from 300 applicants; personnel folder improvements underway.  \n- **Technology and Communication Systems:** Teamworks use is being pushed for compliance and scheduling; software inventory and ITS communication streamlined; email etiquette reminders issued.  \n- **Compliance and Athlete Status Management:** Litigation-affected athletes managed carefully; eligibility and roster status being clarified; compliance experts are main contacts for questions.  \n- **Strategic Leadership and Department Culture:** Leaders promote unity, presence, and empathy; stress breaking silos and being active with staff and athletes during transitions.  \n- **Upcoming Meetings and Follow-Ups:** Unit leaders to update annual report status; mandatory student employee meeting Thursday; next department meeting skipped due to leadership absence.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Kevin Robinson**\nKevin Robinson to continue filling in for baseball until Aaron Burgagen star