{"parentUuid":"c1f70274-0f41-44b8-9d65-8986ce0e9e7a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JNUZ6PEf7JnpBZ11yiHj9P","type":"tool_result","content":"# Meeting Record — Data / Technology / Software Proposal\n\n**Date:** 2026-08-13, 4:16 PM Central · 39.5 min · MS Teams\n**Source:** Fireflies transcript `01KZYFQ9F92NHG57AHSQJ2DFR4` (476 sentences, full text reviewed)\n**Speakers:** Kenny Boyd (chair), Micah Lamb (ITS), Jon Allen (ITS), Lisa Creed,\nErin Boyette (Business Office), Cody Hall (Athletics Business Office),\nJovon Overshown (Athletics)\n\n> Note: the auto-summary names \"Aaron\" and \"Carrie.\" The actual speaker is **Erin\n> Boyette**; **Carrie** is referenced but did not speak. Eric does not appear in the\n> speaker list and is referred to in the third person (\"Coach Rasch\").\n\n---\n\n## Headline outcome\n\n**Micah Lamb is writing a counter-proposal and sending it to the same group.**\n\nHis position, stated directly: the problems are real and the content is good, but he\ndoes not want a **parallel** framework or flow.\n\n> \"I really don't think we need to go that far to solve a lot of the problems that\n> we're seeing today. If we instead had a more standardized intake and a more\n> standardized framework around the requests coming from athletics, that would really\n> streamline and speed up the path through the existing process… That's where I think\n> we will see most of our gain for less effort.\"\n\nHe was complimentary throughout — \"I really like it,\" \"I like where it's coming\nfrom,\" \"it's not a lot, man\" — so this reads as a scoping disagreement, not a\nrejection. The question he is answering is *how much new process is warranted*, and\nhis answer is: less than the framework proposes.\n\n**The convergence point was a one-page checklist.** Kenny: \"the more simple and\nconcise, almost like one page checklist.\" Lisa agreed immediately. Micah described\nthe same thing as a worksheet telling requesters what they'll need before review,\nincluding what to get from the vendor early, while the vendor is still selling.\n\n## What validated the framework\n\nFour things in the document were independently named as the fix:\n\n1. **Declaring the data category up front.** Micah's strongest endorsement — this is\n   the single biggest source of delay today. \"That piece alone will shave weeks off\n   the process… that has always been what's taken us the longest.\" Different people\n   call the same data different things; a common language resolves it.\n2. **Standardized intake.** \"The one thing I saw that I really liked in the proposal\n   is standardizing that intake from an athletics perspective.\" He confirmed ITS\n   already does exac#####REMINDER\n# Email Draft — Technology Inventory Reminder (5 non-responders)\n\n**To:** [fill: leads for Golf (M), Tennis (M), Tennis (W), Volleyball, Business Office]\n**CC:** none — deliberately dropped for this first reminder\n**From:** Eric Rash\n**Form:** Athletics Technology — Quick Inventory (5 questions, Microsoft Forms)\n**Deadline in email:** Tuesday, September 29\n**Status:** SENT 2026-09-08 — individual per-unit version, five separate emails.\nEric edited the text by hand before sending, so the sent copy is not identical\nto the body below. Treat this file as the source of the ask, not a transcript.\nWatch for responses; second reminder (with Kenny/Jovon CC'd) only if needed.\n\n---\n\n## Subject\n\n`Still need 3 minutes from you — technology inventory`\n\n---\n\n## Body\n\nHi all,\n\nBack in August I asked every program and unit to list the technology and software\nthey use. Most have come in. Yours hasn't yet, so I want to make sure the ask\ndidn't get buried.\n\nThe form is here: [LINK]\n\nSame five questions as before. Your name and role, your sport or unit, what you're\nusing now, and anything you've stopped using in the past year. About three minutes.\n\nPlease don't look anything up. Whatever you can list off the top of your head is\nenough. Small things count too, like a free app, a vendor portal, or a spreadsheet\nsomething important runs on. Those are the ones I'm most likely to miss.\n\nThis isn't a review of how anything was purchased or set up. I just need to know\nwhat's out there, and right now your unit is a blank space on the list.\n\nIf you'd rather talk it through than type it, reply and I'll come to you.\n\nNew deadline is Tuesday, September 29.\n\nThanks,\nEric\n\nEric Rash\nDirector of Applied Performance | Baylor Athletics\n\n---\n\n## Send checklist — not part of the email\n\n- [ ] **Swa#####MICAH\n# Micah Lamb — Follow-Up Meeting Request & Standing Cadence\n\n**Status:** draft text only. Send from Baylor mail — I don't have Micah's address, and\nthe Google auth on this machine is a personal account with a read-only calendar feed,\nso I can't issue the invite.\n\n---\n\n## Email — SENT Friday 2026-08-14\n\n**Status: sent.** Awaiting Micah's reply on both asks — the 30 minutes next week, and\nthe standing cadence.\n\n**Subject:** `Follow-up from yesterday: 30 minutes next week?`\n\nMicah,\n\nThanks for yesterday. Your point about the proposal going further than it needs to was\nfair, and it's the part that stuck with me.\n\nSo instead of a second process running next to yours, I've drafted a one-page routing\nsheet that sits on our end, ahead of the IDEA form. Three questions. It pins down what\ndata is involved and who inside Athletics has already looked at it, so requests reach\nyour team with that settled.\n\nAny chance of 30 minutes next week to walk through it before you finalize your\nmarkups? Would be a shame to write two versions of the same thing.\n\nTuesday or Wednesday afternoon is easiest for me, but I'll take whatever you've got.\n\nSeparately, Kenny wants me as the point of contact be#### TIA CLAUDE\n@AGENTS.md\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis/deliverables:\nfindings_report.html\nTech_Inventory_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-29.docx\nTech_Inventory_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-29.html\nTech_Inventory_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-29.pdf\nTech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.docx\nTech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.pdf\n\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis/raw:\nQuick_Inventory_export_2026-08-24.xlsx\nQuick_Inventory_export_2026-08-29.xlsx\n#### NCAA_BP CLAUDE/AGENTS\n@AGENTS.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"2c44603f-45cd-4d36-b993-8091d2615d11","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:23:23.093Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Meeting Record — Data / Technology / Software Proposal\n\n**Date:** 2026-08-13, 4:16 PM Central · 39.5 min · MS Teams\n**Source:** Fireflies transcript `01KZYFQ9F92NHG57AHSQJ2DFR4` (476 sentences, full text reviewed)\n**Speakers:** Kenny Boyd (chair), Micah Lamb (ITS), Jon Allen (ITS), Lisa Creed,\nErin Boyette (Business Office), Cody Hall (Athletics Business Office),\nJovon Overshown (Athletics)\n\n> Note: the auto-summary names \"Aaron\" and \"Carrie.\" The actual speaker is **Erin\n> Boyette**; **Carrie** is referenced but did not speak. Eric does not appear in the\n> speaker list and is referred to in the third person (\"Coach Rasch\").\n\n---\n\n## Headline outcome\n\n**Micah Lamb is writing a counter-proposal and sending it to the same group.**\n\nHis position, stated directly: the problems are real and the content is good, but he\ndoes not want a **parallel** framework or flow.\n\n> \"I really don't think we need to go that far to solve a lot of the problems that\n> we're seeing today. If we instead had a more standardized intake and a more\n> standardized framework around the requests coming from athletics, that would really\n> streamline and speed up the path through the existing process… That's where I think\n> we will see most of our gain for less effort.\"\n\nHe was complimentary throughout — \"I really like it,\" \"I like where it's coming\nfrom,\" \"it's not a lot, man\" — so this reads as a scoping disagreement, not a\nrejection. The question he is answering is *how much new process is warranted*, and\nhis answer is: less than the framework proposes.\n\n**The convergence point was a one-page checklist.** Kenny: \"the more simple and\nconcise, almost like one page checklist.\" Lisa agreed immediately. Micah described\nthe same thing as a worksheet telling requesters what they'll need before review,\nincluding what to get from the vendor early, while the vendor is still selling.\n\n## What validated the framework\n\nFour things in the document were independently named as the fix:\n\n1. **Declaring the data category up front.** Micah's strongest endorsement — this is\n   the single biggest source of delay today. \"That piece alone will shave weeks off\n   the process… that has always been what's taken us the longest.\" Different people\n   call the same data different things; a common language resolves it.\n2. **Standardized intake.** \"The one thing I saw that I really liked in the proposal\n   is standardizing that intake from an athletics perspective.\" He confirmed ITS\n   already does exac#####REMINDER\n# Email Draft — Technology Inventory Reminder (5 non-responders)\n\n**To:** [fill: leads for Golf (M), Tennis (M), Tennis (W), Volleyball, Business Office]\n**CC:** none — deliberately dropped for this first reminder\n**From:** Eric Rash\n**Form:** Athletics Technology — Quick Inventory (5 questions, Microsoft Forms)\n**Deadline in email:** Tuesday, September 29\n**Status:** SENT 2026-09-08 — individual per-unit version, five separate emails.\nEric edited the text by hand before sending, so the sent copy is not identical\nto the body below. Treat this file as the source of the ask, not a transcript.\nWatch for responses; second reminder (with Kenny/Jovon CC'd) only if needed.\n\n---\n\n## Subject\n\n`Still need 3 minutes from you — technology inventory`\n\n---\n\n## Body\n\nHi all,\n\nBack in August I asked every program and unit to list the technology and software\nthey use. Most have come in. Yours hasn't yet, so I want to make sure the ask\ndidn't get buried.\n\nThe form is here: [LINK]\n\nSame five questions as before. Your name and role, your sport or unit, what you're\nusing now, and anything you've stopped using in the past year. About three minutes.\n\nPlease don't look anything up. Whatever you can list off the top of your head is\nenough. Small things count too, like a free app, a vendor portal, or a spreadsheet\nsomething important runs on. Those are the ones I'm most likely to miss.\n\nThis isn't a review of how anything was purchased or set up. I just need to know\nwhat's out there, and right now your unit is a blank space on the list.\n\nIf you'd rather talk it through than type it, reply and I'll come to you.\n\nNew deadline is Tuesday, September 29.\n\nThanks,\nEric\n\nEric Rash\nDirector of Applied Performance | Baylor Athletics\n\n---\n\n## Send checklist — not part of the email\n\n- [ ] **Swa#####MICAH\n# Micah Lamb — Follow-Up Meeting Request & Standing Cadence\n\n**Status:** draft text only. Send from Baylor mail — I don't have Micah's address, and\nthe Google auth on this machine is a personal account with a read-only calendar feed,\nso I can't issue the invite.\n\n---\n\n## Email — SENT Friday 2026-08-14\n\n**Status: sent.** Awaiting Micah's reply on both asks — the 30 minutes next week, and\nthe standing cadence.\n\n**Subject:** `Follow-up from yesterday: 30 minutes next week?`\n\nMicah,\n\nThanks for yesterday. Your point about the proposal going further than it needs to was\nfair, and it's the part that stuck with me.\n\nSo instead of a second process running next to yours, I've drafted a one-page routing\nsheet that sits on our end, ahead of the IDEA form. Three questions. It pins down what\ndata is involved and who inside Athletics has already looked at it, so requests reach\nyour team with that settled.\n\nAny chance of 30 minutes next week to walk through it before you finalize your\nmarkups? Would be a shame to write two versions of the same thing.\n\nTuesday or Wednesday afternoon is easiest for me, but I'll take whatever you've got.\n\nSeparately, Kenny wants me as the point of contact be#### TIA CLAUDE\n@AGENTS.md\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis/deliverables:\nfindings_report.html\nTech_Inventory_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-29.docx\nTech_Inventory_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-29.html\nTech_Inventory_EXEC_SUMMARY_ER_2026-08-29.pdf\nTech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.docx\nTech_Inventory_FINDINGS_ER_2026-08-29.pdf\n\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Tech_Inventory_Analysis/raw:\nQuick_Inventory_export_2026-08-24.xlsx\nQuick_Inventory_export_2026-08-29.xlsx\n#### NCAA_BP CLAUDE/AGENTS\n@AGENTS.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c1f70274-0f41-44b8-9d65-8986ce0e9e7a","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"2c44603f-45cd-4d36-b993-8091d2615d11","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf4MGrct8eBQv1ZWuvHPg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0126gQF35Ue4XNFkXFck2T5Z","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/DEV/RTP_Constraints_Course && head -c 3000 MISSION.md; echo \"#### CLAUDE\"; head -c 2500 CLAUDE.md; echo \"#### lessons\"; ls lessons interviews learning-records docs/handoffs; echo \"#### INTERVIEW head\"; head -c 1500 INTERVIEW.md; echo; grep -rnEi 'deadline|due|by (oct|nov|dec|sept)|joe (smith|reed)|jacob|tim ' MISSION.md NOTES.md INTERVIEW.md docs/handoffs/*.md 2>/dev/null | cut -c1-250 | head -30","description":"Read RTP course mission, lessons, handoffs"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19172,"cache_read_input_tokens":93255,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19172,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0126gQF35Ue4XNFkXFck2T5Z":{"command":"cd ~/DEV/RTP_Constraints_Course && head -c 3000 MISSION.md; echo \"#### CLAUDE\"; head -c 2500 CLAUDE.md; echo \"#### lessons\"; ls lessons interviews learning-records docs/handoffs; echo \"#### INTERVIEW head\"; head -c 1500 INTERVIEW.md; echo; grep -rnEi 'deadline|due|by (oct|nov|dec|sept)|joe (smith|reed)|jacob|tim ' MISSION.md NOTES.md INTERVIEW.md docs/handoffs/*.md 2>/dev/null | cut -c1-250 | head -30","description":"Read RTP course mission, lessons, handoffs"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011Cf4MGqnXAV5HgaeqP56tc","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"977bcb8a-6236-499a-9617-fcbcd1fe5ff9","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:23:25.792Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"977bcb8a-6236-499a-9617-fcbcd1fe5ff9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0126gQF35Ue4XNFkXFck2T5Z","type":"tool_result","content":"# Mission: Constraints-Led Return to Performance (American Football)\n\n> **Confirmed with Joe on 2026-09-03**, in a 47.8-minute mission interview run from\n> `INTERVIEW.md`. Every claim below traces to something Joe said. Source and timestamps:\n> `interviews/2026-09-03-joe-smith-mission-interview.md`.\n> Quoted lines are Joe's own words.\n\n## Why\n\nJoe Smith (assistant athletic trainer, Baylor Athletics) wants to own return to\nperformance at Baylor football. In his words: *\"I want to be known as the guy in this\nroom that does return to performance… and that I make our football team better by the\nreturn to performance that the guys complete.\"*\n\nHe draws a hard line between **return to play** and **return to performance**. Return to\nplay is when drills begin to look and feel like football. Return to performance is when\nyou are chasing back the level the player actually held before. His test for it: *\"I\ncould go out there and play football. I would not be expected to perform at the same\nlevel that our guys are.\"* He wants that distinction written into the Baylor framework,\nand says the sheet already in use has room for it.\n\nWhat he is not asking for is a way to fill the post-clearance gap. **He explicitly\nrejected that boundary.** The work starts at the back end of rehab, before clearance, and\nit also covers acute cases — players who are never held out at all, which is most of what\nhe does day to day.\n\nThe method he already runs is a needs assessment, then a **reverse calendar** worked\nbackwards from the target event, then density and intensity set per day. That method is\nsound and stays. What it lacks is a defensible account of *why* a given exposure is the\nright one — and that is the gap this course fills.\n\nThe sharpest thing Joe said about his own weakness: *\"I'm least confident in the ability\nto rationalize a very subjective process to objectively oriented people.\"*\n\n**That sentence is the mission.** Joe does not need convincing that this work matters. He\nneeds language, structure and evidence that survive a hostile question from someone who\nwas not there.\n\n## Success looks like\n\n- Joe can explain to a skeptical position coach why a pre-planned cone drill is not evidence of readiness.\n- **Joe can defend a subjective judgement to an objective audience** — naming the information the athlete had to use, the problem the exposure posed, and why the result counts as evidence. This is the one that matters most to him.\n- Joe can name the three constraint classes — performer, task, environment — and deliberately manipulate each to raise or lower a drill's demand.\n- **Joe can say why his non-reactive drills flatter an athlete**, and design the reactive exposure that finds what they hide. He has already seen this happen with a returning offensive lineman; he does not yet have the vocabulary for it.\n- Joe can state, for a given position and injury, what perceptual information that job actually requires, and judge whether a drill samples it (represent#### CLAUDE\n# RTP_Constraints_Course\n\nA **teaching workspace**, not a code project. It runs on Matt Pocock's `teach` skill\n(mattpocock-skills 1.2.3).\n\n**Learner:** Joe Smith, assistant athletic trainer, Baylor Athletics.\n**Sponsor:** Eric Rash.\n**Topic:** ecological dynamics and the constraints-led approach (CLA) applied to the last\nphase of return to performance in American football.\n\n## How to work here\n\nType `/teach` in this directory. Do not hand-roll lessons — the skill owns the workflow.\n`teach` and `ask-matt` are `disable-model-invocation: true`, so **a human must type the\nslash command**; the agent cannot invoke them.\n\nRead before doing anything: `MISSION.md`, then `NOTES.md` (open questions and framing\nwrinkles), then `RESOURCES.md`.\n\n## Layout\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `MISSION.md` | Why Joe is learning this. Grounds every teaching decision. |\n| `INTERVIEW.md` | **Mission interview to run with Joe. Do this before lesson 0001.** 45 min; Parts 1 and 2 are the ones that matter. Run 2026-09-03. |\n| `interviews/` | Transcripts of interviews actually run, plus a key-answers digest. Joe's mission interview lives here. **Names athletes and their injuries — never commit, never paste into an artifact or an external service.** |\n| `RESOURCES.md` | Curated trusted sources. Most are already in Eric's SecondBrain library. |\n| `NOTES.md` | Open questions, preferences, working notes. |\n| `assets/` | Reusable lesson components. `course.css` is shared by every lesson. |\n| `lessons/` | `NNNN-slug.html` — the unit of teaching. 0001 written 2026-09-03 (opposed vs unopposed). |\n| `reference/` | `*.html` cheat sheets. The compressed residue of lessons. |\n| `reference/gray-infographics/` | Local copies of Rob Gray's free infographics, saved 2026-09-03 because the site is one person's. Includes the 21-study comparative table, extracted and searchable. **Do not republish, do not commit.** See its `README.md`. |\n| `sources/` | Full-text books and long PDFs obtained legitimately. Author-supplied — **never redistribute, never commit.** Holds the Myszka book (240 pages, printed 14–253), its OCR text sidecar, the reversible scan manifest, and the extracted bibliography. |\n| `scripts/` | The book-scan pipeline. `scan_pipeline.py` turns phone photos into a page-ordered OCR'd PDF and **refuses to rename unless the arithmetic gate passes**; `verify_pdf.py` proves page order afterwards by an independent route. Read `scan_pipeline.py`'s docstring before the next scanning job #### lessons\ndocs/handoffs:\n2026-09-02-research-phase-complete.md\n2026-09-03-book-scan-pipeline.md\n2026-09-03-gray-book-and-joe-prep.md\n2026-09-03-lesson-0001-shipped.md\n2026-09-05-book-scan-complete.md\n2026-09-10-myszka-book-rebuild.md\n\ninterviews:\n2026-09-03-joe-smith-mission-interview.md\n\nlearning-records:\n0001-lesson-0001-hook-opposed-vs-unopposed.md\n\nlessons:\n0001-opposed-vs-unopposed.html\n#### INTERVIEW head\n# Mission interview — Eric and Joe\n\n**Purpose:** confirm or rewrite `MISSION.md`, which is currently **DRAFT and entirely inferred\nfrom Eric's spoken brief.** Every \"Why\" line and every \"Success looks like\" bullet in it is a guess.\n**Until this conversation happens, the course is being built for a person nobody has asked.**\n\n**Time:** 45 minutes. **Do not** try to cover everything — Part 1 and Part 2 are the ones that\nmatter; the rest can happen over a second coffee.\n\n**One rule: ask, then shut up.** The failure mode here is Eric describing the course and Joe\nagreeing. Joe agreeing is not information. **If Joe's answer to a question is a version of what\nEric already wrote, ask \"what would make that wrong?\" before moving on.**\n\n**Take notes verbatim where you can.** Joe's own words about his job are worth more than a clean\nparaphrase — the lessons get written in his language, not ours.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — Does Joe want this? (MUST. Ask first. Do not skip.)\n\n**This is the question Eric is most likely to skip, and the one that decides whether anything else\nis worth doing.** The whole project assumes Joe wants to own this niche. **Nobody has checked.**\n\n1. **\"What do you want to be known for here in three years?\"**\n   *Open, not leading. Do not say \"return to performance\" first — see if he does.*\n\n2. **\"When a player is medically cleared but obviously isn't right yet — whose problem is that\n   here, currently?\"**\n   *Tests whether the gap this course targets is a gap Jo\nMISSION.md:10:Joe Smith (assistant athletic trainer, Baylor Athletics) wants to own return to\nMISSION.md:58:- **Joe does not own medical clearance.** The method has to slot into the existing physician/ATC chain, not replace it. Jacob (director of rehab) and Tim (head athletic trainer) are the gatekeepers.\nINTERVIEW.md:14:**Take notes verbatim where you can.** Joe's own words about his job are worth more than a clean\nNOTES.md:22:**Joe Smith** and the sponsor is **Eric Rash**. `MISSION.md` is written about Joe on\nNOTES.md:213:  numbers, not reproduce long verbatim passages. Do not publish the scan or its text to any","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e7684cbb-20ed-422e-ad9c-1bb6d5036340","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:23:26.036Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Mission: Constraints-Led Return to Performance (American Football)\n\n> **Confirmed with Joe on 2026-09-03**, in a 47.8-minute mission interview run from\n> `INTERVIEW.md`. Every claim below traces to something Joe said. Source and timestamps:\n> `interviews/2026-09-03-joe-smith-mission-interview.md`.\n> Quoted lines are Joe's own words.\n\n## Why\n\nJoe Smith (assistant athletic trainer, Baylor Athletics) wants to own return to\nperformance at Baylor football. In his words: *\"I want to be known as the guy in this\nroom that does return to performance… and that I make our football team better by the\nreturn to performance that the guys complete.\"*\n\nHe draws a hard line between **return to play** and **return to performance**. Return to\nplay is when drills begin to look and feel like football. Return to performance is when\nyou are chasing back the level the player actually held before. His test for it: *\"I\ncould go out there and play football. I would not be expected to perform at the same\nlevel that our guys are.\"* He wants that distinction written into the Baylor framework,\nand says the sheet already in use has room for it.\n\nWhat he is not asking for is a way to fill the post-clearance gap. **He explicitly\nrejected that boundary.** The work starts at the back end of rehab, before clearance, and\nit also covers acute cases — players who are never held out at all, which is most of what\nhe does day to day.\n\nThe method he already runs is a needs assessment, then a **reverse calendar** worked\nbackwards from the target event, then density and intensity set per day. That method is\nsound and stays. What it lacks is a defensible account of *why* a given exposure is the\nright one — and that is the gap this course fills.\n\nThe sharpest thing Joe said about his own weakness: *\"I'm least confident in the ability\nto rationalize a very subjective process to objectively oriented people.\"*\n\n**That sentence is the mission.** Joe does not need convincing that this work matters. He\nneeds language, structure and evidence that survive a hostile question from someone who\nwas not there.\n\n## Success looks like\n\n- Joe can explain to a skeptical position coach why a pre-planned cone drill is not evidence of readiness.\n- **Joe can defend a subjective judgement to an objective audience** — naming the information the athlete had to use, the problem the exposure posed, and why the result counts as evidence. This is the one that matters most to him.\n- Joe can name the three constraint classes — performer, task, environment — and deliberately manipulate each to raise or lower a drill's demand.\n- **Joe can say why his non-reactive drills flatter an athlete**, and design the reactive exposure that finds what they hide. He has already seen this happen with a returning offensive lineman; he does not yet have the vocabulary for it.\n- Joe can state, for a given position and injury, what perceptual information that job actually requires, and judge whether a drill samples it (represent#### CLAUDE\n# RTP_Constraints_Course\n\nA **teaching workspace**, not a code project. It runs on Matt Pocock's `teach` skill\n(mattpocock-skills 1.2.3).\n\n**Learner:** Joe Smith, assistant athletic trainer, Baylor Athletics.\n**Sponsor:** Eric Rash.\n**Topic:** ecological dynamics and the constraints-led approach (CLA) applied to the last\nphase of return to performance in American football.\n\n## How to work here\n\nType `/teach` in this directory. Do not hand-roll lessons — the skill owns the workflow.\n`teach` and `ask-matt` are `disable-model-invocation: true`, so **a human must type the\nslash command**; the agent cannot invoke them.\n\nRead before doing anything: `MISSION.md`, then `NOTES.md` (open questions and framing\nwrinkles), then `RESOURCES.md`.\n\n## Layout\n\n| Path | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `MISSION.md` | Why Joe is learning this. Grounds every teaching decision. |\n| `INTERVIEW.md` | **Mission interview to run with Joe. Do this before lesson 0001.** 45 min; Parts 1 and 2 are the ones that matter. Run 2026-09-03. |\n| `interviews/` | Transcripts of interviews actually run, plus a key-answers digest. Joe's mission interview lives here. **Names athletes and their injuries — never commit, never paste into an artifact or an external service.** |\n| `RESOURCES.md` | Curated trusted sources. Most are already in Eric's SecondBrain library. |\n| `NOTES.md` | Open questions, preferences, working notes. |\n| `assets/` | Reusable lesson components. `course.css` is shared by every lesson. |\n| `lessons/` | `NNNN-slug.html` — the unit of teaching. 0001 written 2026-09-03 (opposed vs unopposed). |\n| `reference/` | `*.html` cheat sheets. The compressed residue of lessons. |\n| `reference/gray-infographics/` | Local copies of Rob Gray's free infographics, saved 2026-09-03 because the site is one person's. Includes the 21-study comparative table, extracted and searchable. **Do not republish, do not commit.** See its `README.md`. |\n| `sources/` | Full-text books and long PDFs obtained legitimately. Author-supplied — **never redistribute, never commit.** Holds the Myszka book (240 pages, printed 14–253), its OCR text sidecar, the reversible scan manifest, and the extracted bibliography. |\n| `scripts/` | The book-scan pipeline. `scan_pipeline.py` turns phone photos into a page-ordered OCR'd PDF and **refuses to rename unless the arithmetic gate passes**; `verify_pdf.py` proves page order afterwards by an independent route. Read `scan_pipeline.py`'s docstring before the next scanning job #### lessons\ndocs/handoffs:\n2026-09-02-research-phase-complete.md\n2026-09-03-book-scan-pipeline.md\n2026-09-03-gray-book-and-joe-prep.md\n2026-09-03-lesson-0001-shipped.md\n2026-09-05-book-scan-complete.md\n2026-09-10-myszka-book-rebuild.md\n\ninterviews:\n2026-09-03-joe-smith-mission-interview.md\n\nlearning-records:\n0001-lesson-0001-hook-opposed-vs-unopposed.md\n\nlessons:\n0001-opposed-vs-unopposed.html\n#### INTERVIEW head\n# Mission interview — Eric and Joe\n\n**Purpose:** confirm or rewrite `MISSION.md`, which is currently **DRAFT and entirely inferred\nfrom Eric's spoken brief.** Every \"Why\" line and every \"Success looks like\" bullet in it is a guess.\n**Until this conversation happens, the course is being built for a person nobody has asked.**\n\n**Time:** 45 minutes. **Do not** try to cover everything — Part 1 and Part 2 are the ones that\nmatter; the rest can happen over a second coffee.\n\n**One rule: ask, then shut up.** The failure mode here is Eric describing the course and Joe\nagreeing. Joe agreeing is not information. **If Joe's answer to a question is a version of what\nEric already wrote, ask \"what would make that wrong?\" before moving on.**\n\n**Take notes verbatim where you can.** Joe's own words about his job are worth more than a clean\nparaphrase — the lessons get written in his language, not ours.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — Does Joe want this? (MUST. Ask first. Do not skip.)\n\n**This is the question Eric is most likely to skip, and the one that decides whether anything else\nis worth doing.** The whole project assumes Joe wants to own this niche. **Nobody has checked.**\n\n1. **\"What do you want to be known for here in three years?\"**\n   *Open, not leading. Do not say \"return to performance\" first — see if he does.*\n\n2. **\"When a player is medically cleared but obviously isn't right yet — whose problem is that\n   here, currently?\"**\n   *Tests whether the gap this course targets is a gap Jo\nMISSION.md:10:Joe Smith (assistant athletic trainer, Baylor Athletics) wants to own return to\nMISSION.md:58:- **Joe does not own medical clearance.** The method has to slot into the existing physician/ATC chain, not replace it. Jacob (director of rehab) and Tim (head athletic trainer) are the gatekeepers.\nINTERVIEW.md:14:**Take notes verbatim where you can.** Joe's own words about his job are worth more than a clean\nNOTES.md:22:**Joe Smith** and the sponsor is **Eric Rash**. `MISSION.md` is written about Joe on\nNOTES.md:213:  numbers, not reproduce long verbatim passages. Do not publish the scan or its text to any","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"977bcb8a-6236-499a-9617-fcbcd1fe5ff9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"e7684cbb-20ed-422e-ad9c-1bb6d5036340","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf4MGrct8eBQv1ZWuvHPg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015XWFirq3qJrXk7uHuLkwvY","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026 && grep -nEi 'manual|HPI|continuity|NCAA|curricul|education|inventory' CONTEXT.md decisions.md JD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md JD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md | cut -c1-350 | head -50; echo \"#### curriculum folders\"; head -c 1500 ~/DEV/CPSS_BaylorSportScience_Curriculum/CLAUDE.md; echo; head -c 1500 ~/DEV/Education_Content_Agent/CLAUDE.md; echo; head -c 800 ~/DEV/TechEd_Prop/CLAUDE.md 2>/dev/null","description":"Grep JD docs and read curriculum project files"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":19172,"cache_read_input_tokens":93255,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":19172,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015XWFirq3qJrXk7uHuLkwvY":{"command":"cd ~/SecondBrain/Projects/Applied-Performance-JD-2026 && grep -nEi 'manual|HPI|continuity|NCAA|curricul|education|inventory' CONTEXT.md decisions.md JD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md JD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md | cut -c1-350 | head -50; echo \"#### curriculum folders\"; head -c 1500 ~/DEV/CPSS_BaylorSportScience_Curriculum/CLAUDE.md; echo; head -c 1500 ~/DEV/Education_Content_Agent/CLAUDE.md; echo; head -c 800 ~/DEV/TechEd_Prop/CLAUDE.md 2>/dev/null","description":"Grep JD docs and read curriculum project files"}},"apiBlockIndex":4,"requestId":"req_011Cf4MGqnXAV5HgaeqP56tc","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"b413b162-4cb7-4535-9100-1f7b0a1fb7ae","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:23:28.981Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b413b162-4cb7-4535-9100-1f7b0a1fb7ae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015XWFirq3qJrXk7uHuLkwvY","type":"tool_result","content":"CONTEXT.md:21:> Example: KR 1.1 — per-sport coach dashboards (FB via AJ, VB direct) on shared data standards; the HPI protocol and continuity plan are what standardize the underneath. (Resolved 2026-07-17.)\ndecisions.md:77:## 0006 — Education engine reset: 2 module-anchored issues, launch decoupled from website and from approval\ndecisions.md:83:**Decision:** KR 2.1 resets to 2 issues by Aug 31 — Issue #1 by ~Aug 1 anchored by the sleep education module, Issue #2 by ~Aug 22 anchored by the GLP-1 brief, both modules circulated to named stakeholders. The proof-of-concept goes out advance-notice style (\"distributing Aug 1 unless changes requested\"), and launch ships by email t\ndecisions.md:101:**Consequences:** The memo announces the structure as designed system, not stretched deadline. Also resolved this turn: tech decision matrix is the completed Q1 flagship (applied to the live Firstbeat 10-license decision), HPI protocol v1 is the drafted item (agenda for the proactive Kenny meeting), continuity plan stages to Fall. \ndecisions.md:115:**Consequences:** Eric's personal lanes (HPI protocol + dashboarding, tech vetting, white-paper analysis, one-off report/dashboard creation) and the delegated sport delivery reinforce rather than compete: the HPI protocol and continuity plan are the standardization instrument the per-sport dashboards sit on.\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:14:Thanks for the responsibilities document and for the conversation on the 9th. I'm on board with the direction it sets — the department oversight, BAIR, HPI, and technology lanes are exactly where I believe Applied Performance can deliver the most value, and I appreciate the clarity it puts on paper. Below: how I pl\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:22:- **HPI protocol v1 drafted** — data integration, storage, dashboarding, and interpretation — circulated to you and Kenny for feedback by Aug 31. I'd like to walk Kenny through it directly; I'll get that meeting scheduled.\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:24:- **Education engine launched** — first newsletter issue out ~Aug 1 anchored by a sleep education module, second ~Aug 22 anchored by a GLP-1/performance brief. Proof-of-concept issue comes to you and key stakeholders the week before with a stated ship date — flag anything you want changed. Distribution starts on em\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:27:**From September:** I'm proposing we move department OKRs to **semester cycles** aligned to the athletic calendar — Fall (Sep–Dec) and Spring (Jan–May) — with a formal mid-semester checkpoint (~Oct 15, ~Mar 1) where every key result gets scored on-track/off-track. Infrastructure work like the HPI build and the data\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:33:- **Proactive supervisor check-ins** — I'll initiate the research/HPI conversation with Kenny and the H&W technology-guidance conversations rather than waiting on invitations.\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:11:- **I accept the document as written.** The lanes it defines — department oversight, BAIR, HPI, technology, data infrastructure — are where Applied Performance delivers the most value, and the staffing structure (AJ owning football day-to-day, Noah developing under guidance) is already working in practice.\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:12:- **One structural suggestion:** several responsibilities overlap as drafted — \"Technology oversight,\" \"HPI data integration,\" and \"Create system and infrastructure for data management\" are three entries describing one connected build (data flows in → is governed and stored → is visualized and interpreted). \nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:22:| 4 | **Budget:** is there an AP budget line I manage for technology, education materials, and PD? | A defined annual line I manage with your visibility, spends over an agreed threshold routed to you. If no line exists, I'd like to scope one for FY27. |\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:32:| **This week** | Linda conversation scheduled; Kenny walkthrough of HPI protocol scheduled (I'll initiate both). |\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:33:| **Aug 1** | Newsletter Issue #1 ships (sleep education module) — proof-of-concept to you and stakeholders the week prior with stated ship date. |\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:37:| **Aug 31** | Technology decision matrix documented and to you for sign-off · HPI protocol v1 circulated to you and Kenny · BAIR steering committee organized with documented vetting process · one academic partnership formalized · white paper draft complete (sole ownership following Blake's departure). |\n#### curriculum folders\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nGuidance for Claude Code when working in this repository.\n\n## What this is\n\nCurriculum **content** for the Baylor Athletics Applied Performance internship. Prose\nand doctrine only — there is no code here. Split out of `~/DEV/AgentAssist` on\n2026-08-18 so that curriculum edits and plugin edits stop churning each other.\n\nRead `README.md` first for the layout and the split.\n\n## The sibling repo\n\n`~/DEV/AgentAssist` owns the machine: the question ledger, the agents, the\ndeterministic `tools/` layer, and the competency taxonomy. Paths written as\n`AgentAssist/context/competencies.md` refer to that repo, not this one.\n\nTwo files there are load-bearing for anything written here:\n\n- `AgentAssist/context/competencies.md` — the eight domain codes (`AM`, `NM`, `SP`,\n  `DL`, `RTP`, `COM`, `RM`, `OPS`). Every module tags against these. **Renaming a code\n  is a breaking change** across both repos — grep before editing.\n- `AgentAssist/context/department.md` — department facts, including `<!-- TODO -->`\n  markers for things only the department can supply. Flag a blocking TODO rather than\n  substituting a plausible value.\n\nThe agent that edits this repo is `AgentAssist/agents/curriculum-architect.md`.\n\n## Rules that survive the split\n\n**Keep the program lean.** Applied Performance is one person covering every sport with\n1–2 rolling interns. Anything that costs Eric keyboard time is a tax on the whole\ndepartment. A module that requires him to build something is a module t\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nGuidance for Claude Code when working in this repository.\n\n## What this is\n\nA content engine for Baylor Athletics Applied Performance staff education. A topic and\nan angle go in; a sourced brief comes out; formatted content is written from that brief.\n\nRead `README.md` for the layout, then `context/house-style.md` before writing any\ncontent. The house style is not a suggestion — it was written by the department and the\nexisting decks follow it.\n\n## The boundary that defines this project\n\n**This project researches and writes. It does not render.**\n\n| Step | Owner |\n|---|---|\n| Research the topic, source every claim | `agents/content-researcher.md` here |\n| Write the narrative into house format | `agents/content-writer.md` here |\n| Build the `.pptx` | `five-slides-generator` skill |\n| Build the printed PDF | `html-to-pdf` skill + the Baylor CSS in `~/DEV/TechEd_Prop` |\n\nReuse beats rebuild. AgentAssist once grew a CSV parser that was a worse copy of a\npipeline that already existed, and deleting it was the right call. A pptxgenjs builder\nin this repo would be the same mistake. If a rendering step is missing something, fix\nthe skill, not this repo.\n\n## Research standards\n\n**Every number in a deck comes from a source.** Not from recall, not from a plausible\nfigure. A brief with an unsourced claim is not finished.\n\nSearch order, and it matters:\n\n1. **The user's Zotero library first** — via the `sports-science-research-grounding`\n   skill. Content that cites papers \n@AGENTS.md","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b618a10c-3f16-4f66-925d-3fa14791b036","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:23:30.238Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"CONTEXT.md:21:> Example: KR 1.1 — per-sport coach dashboards (FB via AJ, VB direct) on shared data standards; the HPI protocol and continuity plan are what standardize the underneath. (Resolved 2026-07-17.)\ndecisions.md:77:## 0006 — Education engine reset: 2 module-anchored issues, launch decoupled from website and from approval\ndecisions.md:83:**Decision:** KR 2.1 resets to 2 issues by Aug 31 — Issue #1 by ~Aug 1 anchored by the sleep education module, Issue #2 by ~Aug 22 anchored by the GLP-1 brief, both modules circulated to named stakeholders. The proof-of-concept goes out advance-notice style (\"distributing Aug 1 unless changes requested\"), and launch ships by email t\ndecisions.md:101:**Consequences:** The memo announces the structure as designed system, not stretched deadline. Also resolved this turn: tech decision matrix is the completed Q1 flagship (applied to the live Firstbeat 10-license decision), HPI protocol v1 is the drafted item (agenda for the proactive Kenny meeting), continuity plan stages to Fall. \ndecisions.md:115:**Consequences:** Eric's personal lanes (HPI protocol + dashboarding, tech vetting, white-paper analysis, one-off report/dashboard creation) and the delegated sport delivery reinforce rather than compete: the HPI protocol and continuity plan are the standardization instrument the per-sport dashboards sit on.\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:14:Thanks for the responsibilities document and for the conversation on the 9th. I'm on board with the direction it sets — the department oversight, BAIR, HPI, and technology lanes are exactly where I believe Applied Performance can deliver the most value, and I appreciate the clarity it puts on paper. Below: how I pl\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:22:- **HPI protocol v1 drafted** — data integration, storage, dashboarding, and interpretation — circulated to you and Kenny for feedback by Aug 31. I'd like to walk Kenny through it directly; I'll get that meeting scheduled.\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:24:- **Education engine launched** — first newsletter issue out ~Aug 1 anchored by a sleep education module, second ~Aug 22 anchored by a GLP-1/performance brief. Proof-of-concept issue comes to you and key stakeholders the week before with a stated ship date — flag anything you want changed. Distribution starts on em\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:27:**From September:** I'm proposing we move department OKRs to **semester cycles** aligned to the athletic calendar — Fall (Sep–Dec) and Spring (Jan–May) — with a formal mid-semester checkpoint (~Oct 15, ~Mar 1) where every key result gets scored on-track/off-track. Infrastructure work like the HPI build and the data\nJD-feedback-memo-2026-07-20.md:33:- **Proactive supervisor check-ins** — I'll initiate the research/HPI conversation with Kenny and the H&W technology-guidance conversations rather than waiting on invitations.\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:11:- **I accept the document as written.** The lanes it defines — department oversight, BAIR, HPI, technology, data infrastructure — are where Applied Performance delivers the most value, and the staffing structure (AJ owning football day-to-day, Noah developing under guidance) is already working in practice.\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:12:- **One structural suggestion:** several responsibilities overlap as drafted — \"Technology oversight,\" \"HPI data integration,\" and \"Create system and infrastructure for data management\" are three entries describing one connected build (data flows in → is governed and stored → is visualized and interpreted). \nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:22:| 4 | **Budget:** is there an AP budget line I manage for technology, education materials, and PD? | A defined annual line I manage with your visibility, spends over an agreed threshold routed to you. If no line exists, I'd like to scope one for FY27. |\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:32:| **This week** | Linda conversation scheduled; Kenny walkthrough of HPI protocol scheduled (I'll initiate both). |\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:33:| **Aug 1** | Newsletter Issue #1 ships (sleep education module) — proof-of-concept to you and stakeholders the week prior with stated ship date. |\nJD-response-deliverable-2026-07-20.md:37:| **Aug 31** | Technology decision matrix documented and to you for sign-off · HPI protocol v1 circulated to you and Kenny · BAIR steering committee organized with documented vetting process · one academic partnership formalized · white paper draft complete (sole ownership following Blake's departure). |\n#### curriculum folders\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nGuidance for Claude Code when working in this repository.\n\n## What this is\n\nCurriculum **content** for the Baylor Athletics Applied Performance internship. Prose\nand doctrine only — there is no code here. Split out of `~/DEV/AgentAssist` on\n2026-08-18 so that curriculum edits and plugin edits stop churning each other.\n\nRead `README.md` first for the layout and the split.\n\n## The sibling repo\n\n`~/DEV/AgentAssist` owns the machine: the question ledger, the agents, the\ndeterministic `tools/` layer, and the competency taxonomy. Paths written as\n`AgentAssist/context/competencies.md` refer to that repo, not this one.\n\nTwo files there are load-bearing for anything written here:\n\n- `AgentAssist/context/competencies.md` — the eight domain codes (`AM`, `NM`, `SP`,\n  `DL`, `RTP`, `COM`, `RM`, `OPS`). Every module tags against these. **Renaming a code\n  is a breaking change** across both repos — grep before editing.\n- `AgentAssist/context/department.md` — department facts, including `<!-- TODO -->`\n  markers for things only the department can supply. Flag a blocking TODO rather than\n  substituting a plausible value.\n\nThe agent that edits this repo is `AgentAssist/agents/curriculum-architect.md`.\n\n## Rules that survive the split\n\n**Keep the program lean.** Applied Performance is one person covering every sport with\n1–2 rolling interns. Anything that costs Eric keyboard time is a tax on the whole\ndepartment. A module that requires him to build something is a module t\n# CLAUDE.md\n\nGuidance for Claude Code when working in this repository.\n\n## What this is\n\nA content engine for Baylor Athletics Applied Performance staff education. A topic and\nan angle go in; a sourced brief comes out; formatted content is written from that brief.\n\nRead `README.md` for the layout, then `context/house-style.md` before writing any\ncontent. The house style is not a suggestion — it was written by the department and the\nexisting decks follow it.\n\n## The boundary that defines this project\n\n**This project researches and writes. It does not render.**\n\n| Step | Owner |\n|---|---|\n| Research the topic, source every claim | `agents/content-researcher.md` here |\n| Write the narrative into house format | `agents/content-writer.md` here |\n| Build the `.pptx` | `five-slides-generator` skill |\n| Build the printed PDF | `html-to-pdf` skill + the Baylor CSS in `~/DEV/TechEd_Prop` |\n\nReuse beats rebuild. AgentAssist once grew a CSV parser that was a worse copy of a\npipeline that already existed, and deleting it was the right call. A pptxgenjs builder\nin this repo would be the same mistake. If a rendering step is missing something, fix\nthe skill, not this repo.\n\n## Research standards\n\n**Every number in a deck comes from a source.** Not from recall, not from a plausible\nfigure. A brief with an unsourced claim is not finished.\n\nSearch order, and it matters:\n\n1. **The user's Zotero library first** — via the `sports-science-research-grounding`\n   skill. Content that cites papers \n@AGENTS.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b413b162-4cb7-4535-9100-1f7b0a1fb7ae","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
