{"parentUuid":"9ecff23e-a43e-459a-a1b5-165876177817","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf4MaswGATm4meVYFNiRu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0127LPXGyiybopTLd1xuuG4t","name":"mcp__claude_ai_Fireflies__fireflies_search","input":{"query":"keyword:\"customer discovery\" from:2026-09-01 to:2026-09-03 limit:5","format":"text"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10403,"cache_read_input_tokens":131612,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10403,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0127LPXGyiybopTLd1xuuG4t":{"query":"keyword:\"customer discovery\" from:2026-09-01 to:2026-09-03 limit:5","format":"text"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cf4MaronZTmAV7wbGNY6Y","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"61fc0c68-01f4-47a5-9386-11eeeadbd25f","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:15.291Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"9ecff23e-a43e-459a-a1b5-165876177817","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PSwwXwazAUtSK653WPPSBE","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"keyword:\\\"NCAA\\\" from:2026-09-08 to:2026-09-10 limit:5\",\n  \"results_count\": 1,\n  \"results\": \"[1] Id: 01M23EEHZ3CRE03AYYADWA3N0D\\nTitle: NCAA SSI Webinar — Performance Tech & Data Governance\\nDateString: 2026-09-09T16:01:39.247Z\\nDuration: 45.290000915527344\\nOrganizer Email: eric.rash11@gmail.com\\nMeeting Link: No meeting link\\nSummary: Short Summary: Responsible performance technology in collegiate athletics requires balancing innovation with student-athlete health, safety, and mental well-being. Recommendations emphasized thoughtful policies, education, secure data management, athlete consent, stakeholder feedback, and regular review. Wearables, equipment sensors, optical systems, lab-grade tools, and personal surveys can measure physical and subjective factors, but they must support—not replace—context-aware judgment. Written plans should address validity, reliability, ownership, access, usage, and unintended consequences. Legal, IT, and risk-management review is important because privacy laws vary, data may be sensitive, de-identification can fail, and vendors may resell or reuse information. Staff and coaches need training, while athletes should understand collection practices and participation rights. No single metric reliably predicts performance or risk, and opaque algorithms can undermine trust. Integrating medical and performance data may support injury-risk analysis, but technology should be retained only when it produces actionable value through multidisciplinary collaboration and ongoing learning., Keywords: performance technology, student athlete data, data privacy, educational programs, injury prevention, policy development, Action Items: \\n**Tim Coffey**\\nEngage with student athletes to gather feedback on their experiences and perceptions regarding performance technologies and data use (38:18)\\nEncourage continuous education of performance and sports medicine staff on validity, reliability, and interpretation of performance technology data (21:46)\\n\\n**Mark Koberly**\\nDevelop and maintain a written plan/policy framework for performance technology adoption including justification, validity checks, data privacy, and access protocols (13:37)\\nCoordinate with institutional general counsel, IT, and risk management to ascertain legal and security compliance in data collection, storage, and use (31:06)\\nImplement routine reevaluations of performance technology effectiveness based on actionable outcomes and stakeholder feedback (17:22)\\n\\n**Tim Coffey and Mark Koberly**\\nPromote multidisciplinary stakeholder education including coaches and student athletes before deploying new technology (21:46)\\nAdvocate for transparency and consent regarding data collection and use with student athletes to mitigate anxiety and privacy concerns (20:16)\\n\\nMeeting Attendees: No meeting attendees\\nMeeting Info: Silent Meeting: false, Summary Status: processed\\nParticipants: eric.rash11@gmail.com\"\n}"}]}]},"uuid":"126148d4-bf2e-4c6b-be20-cd4848418ed7","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:15.740Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"keyword:\\\"NCAA\\\" from:2026-09-08 to:2026-09-10 limit:5\",\n  \"results_count\": 1,\n  \"results\": \"[1] Id: 01M23EEHZ3CRE03AYYADWA3N0D\\nTitle: NCAA SSI Webinar — Performance Tech & Data Governance\\nDateString: 2026-09-09T16:01:39.247Z\\nDuration: 45.290000915527344\\nOrganizer Email: eric.rash11@gmail.com\\nMeeting Link: No meeting link\\nSummary: Short Summary: Responsible performance technology in collegiate athletics requires balancing innovation with student-athlete health, safety, and mental well-being. Recommendations emphasized thoughtful policies, education, secure data management, athlete consent, stakeholder feedback, and regular review. Wearables, equipment sensors, optical systems, lab-grade tools, and personal surveys can measure physical and subjective factors, but they must support—not replace—context-aware judgment. Written plans should address validity, reliability, ownership, access, usage, and unintended consequences. Legal, IT, and risk-management review is important because privacy laws vary, data may be sensitive, de-identification can fail, and vendors may resell or reuse information. Staff and coaches need training, while athletes should understand collection practices and participation rights. No single metric reliably predicts performance or risk, and opaque algorithms can undermine trust. Integrating medical and performance data may support injury-risk analysis, but technology should be retained only when it produces actionable value through multidisciplinary collaboration and ongoing learning., Keywords: performance technology, student athlete data, data privacy, educational programs, injury prevention, policy development, Action Items: \\n**Tim Coffey**\\nEngage with student athletes to gather feedback on their experiences and perceptions regarding performance technologies and data use (38:18)\\nEncourage continuous education of performance and sports medicine staff on validity, reliability, and interpretation of performance technology data (21:46)\\n\\n**Mark Koberly**\\nDevelop and maintain a written plan/policy framework for performance technology adoption including justification, validity checks, data privacy, and access protocols (13:37)\\nCoordinate with institutional general counsel, IT, and risk management to ascertain legal and security compliance in data collection, storage, and use (31:06)\\nImplement routine reevaluations of performance technology effectiveness based on actionable outcomes and stakeholder feedback (17:22)\\n\\n**Tim Coffey and Mark Koberly**\\nPromote multidisciplinary stakeholder education including coaches and student athletes before deploying new technology (21:46)\\nAdvocate for transparency and consent regarding data collection and use with student athletes to mitigate anxiety and privacy concerns (20:16)\\n\\nMeeting Attendees: No meeting attendees\\nMeeting Info: Silent Meeting: false, Summary Status: processed\\nParticipants: eric.rash11@gmail.com\"\n}"}],"mcpMeta":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"mcp-ff","version":"0.1.0","description":"Fireflies.ai MCP server for meeting transcript access"}}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9ecff23e-a43e-459a-a1b5-165876177817","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"61fc0c68-01f4-47a5-9386-11eeeadbd25f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0127LPXGyiybopTLd1xuuG4t","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"keyword:\\\"customer discovery\\\" from:2026-09-01 to:2026-09-03 limit:5\",\n  \"results_count\": 1,\n  \"results\": \"[1] Id: 01M1H9BW9DYB17W2JE2RZGGYMZ\\nTitle: Applied Performance — Customer Discovery & Content Plan\\nDateString: 2026-09-02T14:46:30.341Z\\nDuration: 72.9800033569336\\nOrganizer Email: eric.rash11@gmail.com\\nMeeting Link: No meeting link\\nSummary: Short Summary: The discussion defined an applied performance role centered on service, education, research, analytics, and adaptable resources that coaches can tailor to their sports, rather than direct athlete-facing work. Students are preparing data for a research-ready system, with rapid proof-of-concept development and possible external expertise to support scaling. A “start shipping” mindset was encouraged to overcome perfectionism and generate feedback. Customer discovery will begin with short, semi-structured interviews of strength coaches, followed by engagement with athletic medicine and administrators; participants will represent multiple sports, and AI tools such as Fireflies, Granola, and Decipher will support transcription and analysis. Content will use two tracks: brief social-media clips for athletes and deeper technical sessions for staff. Riverside and similar tools may automate editing, captioning, and publishing. Initial goals include four videos by the end of September, interview analysis afterward, and four additional videos between mid-October and early November. Adoption depends on addressing technology gaps, leadership support, staff buy-in, and cultural development., Keywords: Applied Performance, Customer Discovery, Educational Content, Data-Driven Metrics, Strength Coaches, AI Tools, Action Items: \\n**Eric Rash**\\nDevelop initial customer discovery interview questions tailored for strength coaches and athletic staff by early September (29:19)\\nSchedule and conduct interviews with a representative sample (~80%) of strength coaches focusing on data needs, readiness questions, and cultural support for applied performance (18:36)\\nCollaborate on creating educational content in two tracks: short actionable pieces for athletes and detailed technical sessions for coaches and staff (35:47)\\nPilot content creation using Riverside or similar tool, including video recording, AI transcription, and clipping, with assistance from student assistants (38:15)\\nCoordinate with internal stakeholders (athletic medicine, coaches, administrators) to understand cultural and budgetary constraints affecting adoption of applied performance initiatives (29:19)\\nBuild out mockup applied performance website and newsletter to serve as content hub (01:06:15)\\nPlan and adhere to a timeline: complete interview phase by late September, analyze data with AI tools, then push out four videos by mid-October to early November (01:07:53)\\nContinue education and culture development initiatives beyond preceptorship role to sustain long-term applied performance integration (01:09:28)\\nAttend follow-up meetings scheduled tentatively for Fridays or midweek after 5 pm to maintain project momentum and accountability (01:11:30)\\n\\n**Meeting Organizer/Unknown Speaker**\\nAssist Eric Rash in refining interview scripts based on initial feedback and provide expertise on interview methodology and qualitative data analysis (19:53)\\nProvide guidance on AI tools for transcription and data synthesis, like Fireflies and Granola, and demonstrate video production workflow using Riverside (38:15)\\nSupport content strategy development focusing on actionable, bite-sized educational content and framework for measuring success (58:00)\\nShare insights from past educational programs (Performance School/Academy) and recommend delivery formats to increase engagement (01:02:30)\\nCollaborate on setting up Canvas course infrastructure and safeguarding content access for internal distribution with optional assignments and quizzes (01:04:30)\\nCommit to meeting and interview scheduling support; offer to sit in on interviews and advise on execution plan and accountability (01:07:53)\\n\\nMeeting Attendees: No meeting attendees\\nMeeting Info: Silent Meeting: false, Summary Status: processed\\nParticipants: eric.rash11@gmail.com\"\n}"}]}]},"uuid":"7d4bb3fb-c754-4ab9-9431-6a9e0f32c714","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:17.428Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"query\": \"keyword:\\\"customer discovery\\\" from:2026-09-01 to:2026-09-03 limit:5\",\n  \"results_count\": 1,\n  \"results\": \"[1] Id: 01M1H9BW9DYB17W2JE2RZGGYMZ\\nTitle: Applied Performance — Customer Discovery & Content Plan\\nDateString: 2026-09-02T14:46:30.341Z\\nDuration: 72.9800033569336\\nOrganizer Email: eric.rash11@gmail.com\\nMeeting Link: No meeting link\\nSummary: Short Summary: The discussion defined an applied performance role centered on service, education, research, analytics, and adaptable resources that coaches can tailor to their sports, rather than direct athlete-facing work. Students are preparing data for a research-ready system, with rapid proof-of-concept development and possible external expertise to support scaling. A “start shipping” mindset was encouraged to overcome perfectionism and generate feedback. Customer discovery will begin with short, semi-structured interviews of strength coaches, followed by engagement with athletic medicine and administrators; participants will represent multiple sports, and AI tools such as Fireflies, Granola, and Decipher will support transcription and analysis. Content will use two tracks: brief social-media clips for athletes and deeper technical sessions for staff. Riverside and similar tools may automate editing, captioning, and publishing. Initial goals include four videos by the end of September, interview analysis afterward, and four additional videos between mid-October and early November. Adoption depends on addressing technology gaps, leadership support, staff buy-in, and cultural development., Keywords: Applied Performance, Customer Discovery, Educational Content, Data-Driven Metrics, Strength Coaches, AI Tools, Action Items: \\n**Eric Rash**\\nDevelop initial customer discovery interview questions tailored for strength coaches and athletic staff by early September (29:19)\\nSchedule and conduct interviews with a representative sample (~80%) of strength coaches focusing on data needs, readiness questions, and cultural support for applied performance (18:36)\\nCollaborate on creating educational content in two tracks: short actionable pieces for athletes and detailed technical sessions for coaches and staff (35:47)\\nPilot content creation using Riverside or similar tool, including video recording, AI transcription, and clipping, with assistance from student assistants (38:15)\\nCoordinate with internal stakeholders (athletic medicine, coaches, administrators) to understand cultural and budgetary constraints affecting adoption of applied performance initiatives (29:19)\\nBuild out mockup applied performance website and newsletter to serve as content hub (01:06:15)\\nPlan and adhere to a timeline: complete interview phase by late September, analyze data with AI tools, then push out four videos by mid-October to early November (01:07:53)\\nContinue education and culture development initiatives beyond preceptorship role to sustain long-term applied performance integration (01:09:28)\\nAttend follow-up meetings scheduled tentatively for Fridays or midweek after 5 pm to maintain project momentum and accountability (01:11:30)\\n\\n**Meeting Organizer/Unknown Speaker**\\nAssist Eric Rash in refining interview scripts based on initial feedback and provide expertise on interview methodology and qualitative data analysis (19:53)\\nProvide guidance on AI tools for transcription and data synthesis, like Fireflies and Granola, and demonstrate video production workflow using Riverside (38:15)\\nSupport content strategy development focusing on actionable, bite-sized educational content and framework for measuring success (58:00)\\nShare insights from past educational programs (Performance School/Academy) and recommend delivery formats to increase engagement (01:02:30)\\nCollaborate on setting up Canvas course infrastructure and safeguarding content access for internal distribution with optional assignments and quizzes (01:04:30)\\nCommit to meeting and interview scheduling support; offer to sit in on interviews and advise on execution plan and accountability (01:07:53)\\n\\nMeeting Attendees: No meeting attendees\\nMeeting Info: Silent Meeting: false, Summary Status: processed\\nParticipants: eric.rash11@gmail.com\"\n}"}],"mcpMeta":{"_meta":{"io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo":{"name":"mcp-ff","version":"0.1.0","description":"Fireflies.ai MCP server for meeting transcript access"}}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"61fc0c68-01f4-47a5-9386-11eeeadbd25f","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7d4bb3fb-c754-4ab9-9431-6a9e0f32c714","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf4MaswGATm4meVYFNiRu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AeTRpJj2mXNDRv93RkXSw1","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/DEV/RTP_WorkingGroup && head -c 1500 Docs/STATUS.md; echo \"### context\"; head -c 2500 Docs/context.md; echo; ls -la Docs/*.docx | awk '{print $6,$7,$8,$9,$10}'; ls ~/DEV/RTP_WorkingGroup; echo \"### 06-03 HPI intro\"; sed -n '/## Summary/,$p' ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings/2026-06-03_RTP_HPI_IntroMeeting.md | head -45; echo \"### PERFORMANCE.md RTP_WorkingGroup row\"; grep -n 'RTP_WorkingGroup\\|RTP_Constraints' ~/DEV/PERFORMANCE.md ~/DEV/PROGRAM.md ~/DEV/RESEARCH.md | cut -c1-400","description":"Read HPI manual working group files and intro meeting"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10403,"cache_read_input_tokens":131612,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10403,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AeTRpJj2mXNDRv93RkXSw1":{"command":"cd ~/DEV/RTP_WorkingGroup && head -c 1500 Docs/STATUS.md; echo \"### context\"; head -c 2500 Docs/context.md; echo; ls -la Docs/*.docx | awk '{print $6,$7,$8,$9,$10}'; ls ~/DEV/RTP_WorkingGroup; echo \"### 06-03 HPI intro\"; sed -n '/## Summary/,$p' ~/SecondBrain/Inbox/meetings/2026-06-03_RTP_HPI_IntroMeeting.md | head -45; echo \"### PERFORMANCE.md RTP_WorkingGroup row\"; grep -n 'RTP_WorkingGroup\\|RTP_Constraints' ~/DEV/PERFORMANCE.md ~/DEV/PROGRAM.md ~/DEV/RESEARCH.md | cut -c1-400","description":"Read HPI manual working group files and intro meeting"}},"apiBlockIndex":3,"requestId":"req_011Cf4MaronZTmAV7wbGNY6Y","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"34cdac23-2660-4cf0-adc2-b2e96306b840","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:19.050Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"34cdac23-2660-4cf0-adc2-b2e96306b840","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AeTRpJj2mXNDRv93RkXSw1","type":"tool_result","content":"# STATUS\n\nRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n\n## Current state\n- Snapshot committed 2026-09-09 during migration to the DEV + GitHub workflow.\n- 2026-09-11: cloud project migration finished. The three Cowork docs (assessment\n  protocols workflow V1, full gaps and solutions, KB overview) now live in\n  `Docs/program/`. The Cowork project holds only `claude/source-of-truth.md`.\n\n## Next\n-\n\n## Open questions\n-\n\n## Gotchas\n-\n### context\n# RTP Working Group — transferred project context\n\nCarried over from the Claude cloud project, Sept 2026.\n\n## Two connected domains\n\nThe work is not one project. It is data infrastructure and reporting for force plate\nathlete monitoring, plus organizational frameworks (OKRs) that standardize assessment,\nbenchmarking, and staff operations across the working group. They constrain each other.\n\nCollaborators are Applied Performance staff. Athlete population is multi-sport.\n\n## Supporting documentation that exists\n\n`context.md` covering project scope and architecture, spec files for each report\ntemplate, and a Word metric appendix covering 12 metrics with plain-language descriptions.\n\n## Current OKR state\n\nOKR development for the Intake/Assessment pillar is underway, using Andy Grove /\nJohn Doerr methodology.\n\n## The timing pressure\n\nNear-term pressure to evaluate current athletes for year-end player valuations while\nsimultaneously establishing the benchmarks that make those evaluations meaningful.\n\nThis is the bootstrap problem: benchmarks have to exist before evaluations mean anything.\nThe phased answer is an emergency assessment sprint, then benchmark development, then\nfull workflow. Do not skip to full workflow.\n\n## Data resolution rules\n\nForceDecks exports contain ambiguities that need explicit rules rather than case-by-case\njudgment. Duplicate same-date entries with different rep counts: default to the\nhigher-rep session.\n\nAsymmetry gets reported with direction, \"X.X% L\" or \"X.X% R\", never magnitude alone.\n\nPlain-language translation of complex metrics is a priority for anything that reaches a\npractitioner or coach.\n\n## Working patterns\n\n- Concise documentation over verbose\n- Build in layers: automation first, then visualization, then integration\n- Fireflies for meeting transcript retrieval, using keyword plus date filter syntax\n\nSep 9 17:46 Docs/Baylor LE\nSep 9 17:46 Docs/Baylor UE\nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline_ORIGINAL.docx \nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline_V2.docx \nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline_V3.docx \nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline.docx \nAGENTS.md\nBackgroundInfo\nCLAUDE.md\nDocs\nHPI_DataDepot.xlsx\nMatt Jordan’s Approach to Force Plate CMJ Metrics for ACL Return-to-Play.docx\nREADME.md\nRTP_ProgressREPORT\n### 06-03 HPI intro\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Standardized Injury Screening:** FMS and Y Balance tests adopted; weekly CMJ recommended to track athlete status effectively.\n\n- **Rehabilitation Framework Introduced:** Clear phases for rehab tailored to injury types, emphasizing ongoing testing for milestone transitions.\n\n- **Data Management Strategy:** Centralized Excel for screening tests; Kitman software for rehab tracking enhances communication between teams.\n\n- **Collaboration Importance Stressed:** Joint efforts between medical and performance staff boost assessment quality and rehab planning.\n\n- **Vision for Athlete Health:** Standardization aims to reduce injuries and improve return-to-play efficiency, supporting overall organizational success.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n**Jeremy**\nFollow up with athletic trainers to ensure they have profiles and access to Vault Hub and necessary apps for autonomous testing (17:13)\nReach out to Kitman team regarding integration of rehab framework and testing data into Kitman dashboards for easier data management (24:52)\nCollaborate with group to refine responsibility assignments and clarify roles related to rehab framework management (51:51)\nCoordinate inventory assessment for FMS and testing equipment across facilities and sports to identify gaps and sourcing needs (57:56)\nArrange or provide FMS training opportunities to standardize scoring and assessment understanding among staff (58:59)\n\n**Medical and Performance Staff**\nPerform FMS, Y Balance anterior reach, and CMJ screenings as defined, with intake and exit FMS, and weekly CMJ monitoring where possible (04:13)\nCoordinate to conduct screening collaboratively when possible, or communicate results and flags clearly when performed separately (07:35)\nEnter screening data (FMS scores, Y Balance) into centralized Excel sheets on Teams to maintain accessible records (09:17)\nAddress flags from screening with further assessment and corrective programming based on clinical best judgment (18:38)\nUse rehab framework checklist as a collaborative live document, updating phase progress and responsibilities regularly to maintain communication within the team (50:20)\nCollect and maintain all injury testing and screening data cohesively, minimizing redundant data entry where possible through electronic integration (21:58)\n\n\n### Keywords\n\nFunctional Movement Screen, Rehabilitation Framework, Injury Screening, Return to Play, Performance Testing, Data Management\n\n### PERFORMANCE.md RTP_WorkingGroup row\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PERFORMANCE.md:42:| `RTP_ProgressReport/` | Force plate return-to-play progress reports for individual athletes; three report styles, JSX/HTML template rendered per athlete. Own `AGENTS.md` + `CLAUDE.md`. Git repo, created 2026-09-14 on Windows. A similarly named folder also sits at `RTP_WorkingGroup/RTP_ProgressREPORT/` — check which is current before editing either. |\n/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PROGRAM.md:24:| `RTP_Constraints_Course/` | Constraints-led return-to-performance course for Joe Smith, plus a planned drill-design tool → own `CLAUDE.md` |","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"6766fb3d-f150-4a39-91f0-0bceca3dfccf","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:20.312Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# STATUS\n\nRead this first. Update it before ending every session.\n\n## Current state\n- Snapshot committed 2026-09-09 during migration to the DEV + GitHub workflow.\n- 2026-09-11: cloud project migration finished. The three Cowork docs (assessment\n  protocols workflow V1, full gaps and solutions, KB overview) now live in\n  `Docs/program/`. The Cowork project holds only `claude/source-of-truth.md`.\n\n## Next\n-\n\n## Open questions\n-\n\n## Gotchas\n-\n### context\n# RTP Working Group — transferred project context\n\nCarried over from the Claude cloud project, Sept 2026.\n\n## Two connected domains\n\nThe work is not one project. It is data infrastructure and reporting for force plate\nathlete monitoring, plus organizational frameworks (OKRs) that standardize assessment,\nbenchmarking, and staff operations across the working group. They constrain each other.\n\nCollaborators are Applied Performance staff. Athlete population is multi-sport.\n\n## Supporting documentation that exists\n\n`context.md` covering project scope and architecture, spec files for each report\ntemplate, and a Word metric appendix covering 12 metrics with plain-language descriptions.\n\n## Current OKR state\n\nOKR development for the Intake/Assessment pillar is underway, using Andy Grove /\nJohn Doerr methodology.\n\n## The timing pressure\n\nNear-term pressure to evaluate current athletes for year-end player valuations while\nsimultaneously establishing the benchmarks that make those evaluations meaningful.\n\nThis is the bootstrap problem: benchmarks have to exist before evaluations mean anything.\nThe phased answer is an emergency assessment sprint, then benchmark development, then\nfull workflow. Do not skip to full workflow.\n\n## Data resolution rules\n\nForceDecks exports contain ambiguities that need explicit rules rather than case-by-case\njudgment. Duplicate same-date entries with different rep counts: default to the\nhigher-rep session.\n\nAsymmetry gets reported with direction, \"X.X% L\" or \"X.X% R\", never magnitude alone.\n\nPlain-language translation of complex metrics is a priority for anything that reaches a\npractitioner or coach.\n\n## Working patterns\n\n- Concise documentation over verbose\n- Build in layers: automation first, then visualization, then integration\n- Fireflies for meeting transcript retrieval, using keyword plus date filter syntax\n\nSep 9 17:46 Docs/Baylor LE\nSep 9 17:46 Docs/Baylor UE\nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline_ORIGINAL.docx \nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline_V2.docx \nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline_V3.docx \nSep 9 17:46 Docs/HPI_RTP_Manual_Outline.docx \nAGENTS.md\nBackgroundInfo\nCLAUDE.md\nDocs\nHPI_DataDepot.xlsx\nMatt Jordan’s Approach to Force Plate CMJ Metrics for ACL Return-to-Play.docx\nREADME.md\nRTP_ProgressREPORT\n### 06-03 HPI intro\n## Summary\n\n### Overview\n\n- **Standardized Injury Screening:** FMS and Y Balance tests adopted; weekly CMJ recommended to track athlete status effectively.\n\n- **Rehabilitation Framework Introduced:** Clear phases for rehab tailored to injury types, emphasizing ongoing testing for milestone transitions.\n\n- **Data Management Strategy:** Centralized Excel for screening tests; Kitman software for rehab tracking enhances communication between teams.\n\n- **Collaboration Importance Stressed:** Joint efforts between medical and performance staff boost assessment quality and rehab planning.\n\n- **Vision for Athlete Health:** Standardization aims to reduce injuries and improve return-to-play efficiency, supporting overall organizational success.\n\n### Action Items\n\n\n*