{"parentUuid":"cc267e85-b230-4cd8-bbdd-a9dfc6b4eb4b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cf4Mc5dFah3BqPF4puxDf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019MwHQdyLcwH3yF45jcX8bg","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"f=\"$HOME/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-BaylorUniversity/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/SSI_Notes_09092026.docx\"; ls -la \"$f\"; textutil -convert txt -stdout \"$f\" 2>/dev/null | head -c 6000","description":"Extract text of the SSI webinar notes docx"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8744,"cache_read_input_tokens":142015,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8744,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_019MwHQdyLcwH3yF45jcX8bg":{"command":"f=\"$HOME/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-BaylorUniversity/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/SSI_Notes_09092026.docx\"; ls -la \"$f\"; textutil -convert txt -stdout \"$f\" 2>/dev/null | head -c 6000","description":"Extract text of the SSI webinar notes docx"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011Cf4Mc4rMxrbp6r7jytc5o","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionMcpServer":"claude.ai Fireflies","attributionMcpTool":"fireflies_search","type":"assistant","uuid":"3f85ad79-7c09-4159-8e5f-35172e1a19f9","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:33.590Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"3f85ad79-7c09-4159-8e5f-35172e1a19f9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019MwHQdyLcwH3yF45jcX8bg","type":"tool_result","content":"-rw-rw----@ 1 kristinarash  staff  23000 Sep 10 09:37 /Users/kristinarash/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-BaylorUniversity/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/SSI_Notes_09092026.docx\nNotes\nResponsible Use of Performance Technologies\nThe responsible use of performance technology in collegiate athletics requires balancing innovation with safeguarding student-athlete health and well-being.\n\t•\tGuidance Focuses on Policy, Education, Data Management, Adoption, and Improvement\n\t•\tThe NCAA Committee developed recommendations to help schools innovate while protecting athletes. \n\t•\tKey areas include thoughtful policy setting, ongoing education, secure data handling, and continuous review. \n\t•\tThe goal is to ensure technologies enhance performance without compromising safety or mental health. \n\t•\tTim Coffey and Mark Coberly contributed directly to crafting these guidelines.\n\t•\tTechnology Must Be Used as a Tool, Not a Solution\n\t•\tTim highlighted that technologies like wearables and sensors provide data but don’t solve problems alone. \n\t•\tMark emphasized asking specific questions before selecting tools to avoid data overload. \n\t•\tBoth agreed that data should guide decisions but not dictate them blindly, respecting individual contexts. \n\t•\tThis mindset helps avoid misuse and supports targeted care and performance improvements.\n\t•\tWritten Plans Are Essential to Manage Technology Use and Avoid Risks\n\t•\tMark described developing policies that require purchasers to justify technology with clear questions. \n\t•\tPlans must address data validity, reliability, usage protocols, data ownership, and access controls. \n\t•\tRegular reevaluation is critical to assess ongoing value, actionable insights, and unintended consequences. \n\t•\tThis structured approach prevents impulse buys driven by peer pressure or hype.\n\t•\tRisks Include Misuse of Data and Mental Health Impacts\n\t•\tMark warned about dangers of ignoring critical data or misinterpreting it, which can harm athletes and institutions. \n\t•\tTim shared research showing data surveillance can cause anxiety and fear among athletes. \n\t•\tMisuse risks increase without clear policies on access, interpretation, and communication. \n\t•\tProactive, transparent education and consent processes help mitigate these risks.\nTypes and Evolution of Performance Technologies\nPerformance technologies span wearable devices, equipment sensors, optical systems, lab-grade tools, and personal surveys, and they keep evolving in use and complexity.\n\t•\tDiverse Tools Capture Physical and Personal Metrics\n\t•\tTim described wearables like heart rate monitors and sweat lactate patches worn by athletes. \n\t•\tEquipment-based tools include tags on bats measuring swing speed. \n\t•\tOptical camera systems track athlete positions and motion metrics on the field. \n\t•\tLab-grade devices such as DEXA scanners and force plates assess body composition and biomechanics. \n\t•\tPersonal surveys track mood, sleep, and menstrual cycles, integrating subjective athlete data.\n\t•\tUse Cases Depend on Team Culture and Implementation\n\t•\tTim shared examples where a scale was used differently across teams, either as a hydration guide or avoided due to body image concerns. \n\t•\tThis illustrates the importance of adapting technology use to athlete needs and cultural context. \n\t•\tCoaches and trainers need to understand the technology’s role rather than blindly applying metrics. \n\t•\tProper use respects psychological safety and enhances meaningful insights.\n\t•\tIncreasing Access to Advanced Tools Raises New Opportunities and Challenges\n\t•\tMore programs now use lab-grade equipment previously limited to elite settings. \n\t•\tThis allows deeper physical profiling but requires careful interpretation and integration. \n\t•\tAwareness of how these tools fit into broader athlete management is growing. \n\t•\tEducation on data validity is essential as usage spreads.\n\t•\tSelf-Report Data Is Often Overlooked but Critical\n\t•\tSurveys and mood tracking capture important personal experiences often missed by physical sensors. \n\t•\tThese data feed into athlete management systems alongside objective measures. \n\t•\tRecognizing their value helps create a fuller picture of athlete health and readiness. \n\t•\tThey require sensitive handling due to privacy concerns.\nData Ownership, Privacy, and Security\nManaging athlete data demands clear policies on ownership, storage, privacy, and access to protect sensitive information.\n\t•\tLegal Complexity Surrounds Data Protection and Privacy\n\t•\tTim emphasized consulting general counsel because state laws vary and HIPAA coverage is unclear. \n\t•\tPhysiological data like heart rate is more likely HIPAA-protected than performance metrics like sprint times. \n\t•\tMany campuses underestimate the sensitivity of collected data and risks of sharing or publicizing it. \n\t•\tDe-identification often fails to fully anonymize athletes, posing re-identification risks.\n\t•\tData Storage and Access Must Meet Institutional Security Standards\n\t•\tUniversities must control where data is stored, who can access it, and how securely it is handled. \n\t•\tCloud storage raises concerns given past incidents with educational platforms. \n\t•\tCollaboration with IT and risk management departments is crucial for safe data governance. \n\t•\tContracts with vendors should be reviewed carefully to understand data resale or secondary use terms.\n\t•\tTransparency with Student Athletes About Data Use and Consent Is Essential\n\t•\tMark stressed the need to educate athletes on what data is collected and who sees it. \n\t•\tAthletes should be able to opt out of wearable or survey data collection where feasible. \n\t•\tSome data, like video tracking, may be unavoidable but still requires clear communication. \n\t•\tThis transparency builds trust and reduces anxiety around data surveillance.\n\t•\tVendor Practices Can Complicate Data Ownership \n\t•\tSome companies offer discounts in exchange for selling collected data, which they may not fully disclose internally. \n\t•\tPurchasing decision-makers must understand these implications for athlete privacy and institutional rep","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"580ac78b-7cd5-4249-8936-389750e03919","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:35.119Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"-rw-rw----@ 1 kristinarash  staff  23000 Sep 10 09:37 /Users/kristinarash/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-BaylorUniversity/00_Primary_Storage_Drive/SSI_Notes_09092026.docx\nNotes\nResponsible Use of Performance Technologies\nThe responsible use of performance technology in collegiate athletics requires balancing innovation with safeguarding student-athlete health and well-being.\n\t•\tGuidance Focuses on Policy, Education, Data Management, Adoption, and Improvement\n\t•\tThe NCAA Committee developed recommendations to help schools innovate while protecting athletes. \n\t•\tKey areas include thoughtful policy setting, ongoing education, secure data handling, and continuous review. \n\t•\tThe goal is to ensure technologies enhance performance without compromising safety or mental health. \n\t•\tTim Coffey and Mark Coberly contributed directly to crafting these guidelines.\n\t•\tTechnology Must Be Used as a Tool, Not a Solution\n\t•\tTim highlighted that technologies like wearables and sensors provide data but don’t solve problems alone. \n\t•\tMark emphasized asking specific questions before selecting tools to avoid data overload. \n\t•\tBoth agreed that data should guide decisions but not dictate them blindly, respecting individual contexts. \n\t•\tThis mindset helps avoid misuse and supports targeted care and performance improvements.\n\t•\tWritten Plans Are Essential to Manage Technology Use and Avoid Risks\n\t•\tMark described developing policies that require purchasers to justify technology with clear questions. \n\t•\tPlans must address data validity, reliability, usage protocols, data ownership, and access controls. \n\t•\tRegular reevaluation is critical to assess ongoing value, actionable insights, and unintended consequences. \n\t•\tThis structured approach prevents impulse buys driven by peer pressure or hype.\n\t•\tRisks Include Misuse of Data and Mental Health Impacts\n\t•\tMark warned about dangers of ignoring critical data or misinterpreting it, which can harm athletes and institutions. \n\t•\tTim shared research showing data surveillance can cause anxiety and fear among athletes. \n\t•\tMisuse risks increase without clear policies on access, interpretation, and communication. \n\t•\tProactive, transparent education and consent processes help mitigate these risks.\nTypes and Evolution of Performance Technologies\nPerformance technologies span wearable devices, equipment sensors, optical systems, lab-grade tools, and personal surveys, and they keep evolving in use and complexity.\n\t•\tDiverse Tools Capture Physical and Personal Metrics\n\t•\tTim described wearables like heart rate monitors and sweat lactate patches worn by athletes. \n\t•\tEquipment-based tools include tags on bats measuring swing speed. \n\t•\tOptical camera systems track athlete positions and motion metrics on the field. \n\t•\tLab-grade devices such as DEXA scanners and force plates assess body composition and biomechanics. \n\t•\tPersonal surveys track mood, sleep, and menstrual cycles, integrating subjective athlete data.\n\t•\tUse Cases Depend on Team Culture and Implementation\n\t•\tTim shared examples where a scale was used differently across teams, either as a hydration guide or avoided due to body image concerns. \n\t•\tThis illustrates the importance of adapting technology use to athlete needs and cultural context. \n\t•\tCoaches and trainers need to understand the technology’s role rather than blindly applying metrics. \n\t•\tProper use respects psychological safety and enhances meaningful insights.\n\t•\tIncreasing Access to Advanced Tools Raises New Opportunities and Challenges\n\t•\tMore programs now use lab-grade equipment previously limited to elite settings. \n\t•\tThis allows deeper physical profiling but requires careful interpretation and integration. \n\t•\tAwareness of how these tools fit into broader athlete management is growing. \n\t•\tEducation on data validity is essential as usage spreads.\n\t•\tSelf-Report Data Is Often Overlooked but Critical\n\t•\tSurveys and mood tracking capture important personal experiences often missed by physical sensors. \n\t•\tThese data feed into athlete management systems alongside objective measures. \n\t•\tRecognizing their value helps create a fuller picture of athlete health and readiness. \n\t•\tThey require sensitive handling due to privacy concerns.\nData Ownership, Privacy, and Security\nManaging athlete data demands clear policies on ownership, storage, privacy, and access to protect sensitive information.\n\t•\tLegal Complexity Surrounds Data Protection and Privacy\n\t•\tTim emphasized consulting general counsel because state laws vary and HIPAA coverage is unclear. \n\t•\tPhysiological data like heart rate is more likely HIPAA-protected than performance metrics like sprint times. \n\t•\tMany campuses underestimate the sensitivity of collected data and risks of sharing or publicizing it. \n\t•\tDe-identification often fails to fully anonymize athletes, posing re-identification risks.\n\t•\tData Storage and Access Must Meet Institutional Security Standards\n\t•\tUniversities must control where data is stored, who can access it, and how securely it is handled. \n\t•\tCloud storage raises concerns given past incidents with educational platforms. \n\t•\tCollaboration with IT and risk management departments is crucial for safe data governance. \n\t•\tContracts with vendors should be reviewed carefully to understand data resale or secondary use terms.\n\t•\tTransparency with Student Athletes About Data Use and Consent Is Essential\n\t•\tMark stressed the need to educate athletes on what data is collected and who sees it. \n\t•\tAthletes should be able to opt out of wearable or survey data collection where feasible. \n\t•\tSome data, like video tracking, may be unavoidable but still requires clear communication. \n\t•\tThis transparency builds trust and reduces anxiety around data surveillance.\n\t•\tVendor Practices Can Complicate Data Ownership \n\t•\tSome companies offer discounts in exchange for selling collected data, which they may not fully disclose internally. \n\t•\tPurchasing decision-makers must understand these implications for athlete privacy and institutional rep","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3f85ad79-7c09-4159-8e5f-35172e1a19f9","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
