:"assistant","uuid":"6ad4b2a5-db97-4e3b-a1fb-3954ff0c2462","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:49.752Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/project-triage","sessionId":"df3b3576-b317-408d-964a-187d57ed18ca","version":"2.1.271","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"6ad4b2a5-db97-4e3b-a1fb-3954ff0c2462","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"d23fcdcc-b354-43a5-a06a-e16fcb59f267","agentId":"a89be5bc70b9389ae","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AR7k6LKNPicsNxciaeb2nA","type":"tool_result","content":"chars: 12438\n5:\t•\tThe NCAA Committee developed recommendations to help schools innovate while protecting athletes. \n14:\t•\tWritten Plans Are Essential to Manage Technology Use and Avoid Risks\n16:\t•\tPlans must address data validity, reliability, usage protocols, data ownership, and access controls. \n25:Performance technologies span wearable devices, equipment sensors, optical systems, lab-grade tools, and personal surveys, and they keep evolving in use and complexity.\n31:\t•\tPersonal surveys track mood, sleep, and menstrual cycles, integrating subjective athlete data.\n43:\t•\tSurveys and mood tracking capture important personal experiences often missed by physical sensors. \n57:\t•\tCollaboration with IT and risk management departments is crucial for safe data governance. \n61:\t•\tAthletes should be able to opt out of wearable or survey data collection where feasible. \n87:\t•\tTim recommended collecting feedback regularly through surveys and direct conversations. \n117:\t•\tThis integration requires cross-department education and coach buy-in. \n127:\t•\tThe NCAA recommendations provide a solid foundation but must evolve with new insights. \n### headings (non-bullet lines) after char 6000\nputation. \nEducation and Stakeholder Engagement\nEducation for all stakeholders is vital to ensure proper use, interpretation, and consent regarding performance technologies.\nMisconceptions and Challenges in Technology Use\nUnderstanding limitations, variability, and the “black box” nature of some performance metrics is key to realistic expectations.\nStrategic Integration and Future Directions\nIntegrating performance technologies with medical records and fostering data-guided decisions will improve athlete outcomes and competitive success.\nKEY TAKEAWAYS OVERVIEW","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c9004c46-280b-4649-9d80-522df47193e8","timestamp":"2026-09-15T01:27:50.978Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"chars: 12438\n5:\t•\tThe NCAA Committee developed recommendations to help schools innovate while protecting athletes. \n14:\t•\tWritten Plans Are Essential to Manage Technology Use and Avoid Risks\n16:\t•\tPlans must address data validity, reliability, usage protocols, data ownership, and access controls. \n25:Performance technologies span wearable devices, equipment sensors, optical systems, lab-grade tools, and personal surveys, and they keep evolving in use and complexity.\n31:\t•\tPersonal surveys track mood, sleep, and menstrual cycles, integrating subjective athlete data.\n43:\t•\tSurveys and mood tracking capture important personal experiences often missed by physical sensors. \n57:\t•\tCollaboration with IT and risk management departments is crucial for safe data governance. \n61:\t•\tAthletes should be able to opt out of wearable or survey data collection where feasible. \n87:\t•\tTim recommended collecting feedback regularly through surveys and direct conversations. \n117:\t•\tThis integration requires cross-department education and coach buy-in. \n127:\t•\tThe NCAA recommendations provide a solid foundation but must evolve with new insights. \n### headings (non-bullet lines) after char 6000\nputation. \nEducation and Stakeholder Engagement\nEducation for all stakeholders is vital to ensure proper use, interpretation, and consent regarding performance technologies.\nMisconceptions and Challenges in Technology Use\nUnderstanding limitations, variability, and the “black box” nature of some performance metrics is key to realistic expectations.\nStrategic Integration and Future Directions\nIntegrating performance technologies with medical records and fostering data-guided decisions will improve athlete outcomes and competitive success.\nKEY TAKEAWAYS OV